window.AB100_QUESTIONS = [{"id":1,"stem":"A company deploys a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that integrates with a Microsoft Power Automate\ndesktop flow.\nYou need to recommend a testing solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Test cases must validate the most recent changes to the agent before the agent is released.\n• The flow must be validated as part of the agent's orchestration.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"A company deploys a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that integrates with a Microsoft Power Automate\ndesktop flow.\nYou need to recommend a testing solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Test cases must validate the most recent changes to the agent before the agent is released.\n• The flow must be validated as part of the agent's orchestration.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Run tests against the latest unpublished version of the agent.\nAdd the flow to the agent as a tool.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nRun tests against the latest unpublished version of the agent.\nThis option is correct because unpublished versions contain the latest updates and configuration changes\nmade to the agent. Microsoft recommends validating these unpublished changes before deployment so that\nissues can be identified early without affecting production users. Testing against the unpublished version\nprovides a safe environment to verify orchestration logic, prompts, integrations, and flow execution before\npublishing the agent to live channels.\nAdd the flow to the agent as a tool.\nThis option is correct because Power Automate flows must be configured as tools for the agent to invoke\nthem during conversations and orchestration workflows. Once added as a tool, the agent can dynamically\ntrigger the flow to perform backend actions, automate processes, or retrieve information. Without adding the\nflow as a tool, the agent cannot use the flow as part of its orchestration capabilities.\nIncorrect Answers:\nPublish the agent to a channel and test the agent on live users.\nThis is incorrect because validation should occur before exposing changes to production users.\nRun tests against the production version of the agent.\nThis is incorrect because the production version does not include the latest unpublished modifications.\nAdd the flow to a canvas app.\nThis is incorrect because canvas apps integrate flows into app interfaces, not into agent orchestration.\nUse the Power Automate for desktop console.\nThis is incorrect because the desktop console is intended for desktop flow management and not for agent\nvalidation.","sourcePages":[2,3],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":2,"stem":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to enhance customer support.\nThe company stores customer data across ServiceNow, Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365\nSupply Chain Management, and Excel files in SharePoint Online.\nYou need to recommend a solution to ensure that the agent can deliver accurate and timely responses. What\nshould you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to enhance customer support.\nThe company stores customer data across ServiceNow, Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365\nSupply Chain Management, and Excel files in SharePoint Online.\nYou need to recommend a solution to ensure that the agent can deliver accurate and timely responses. What\nshould you recommend?\nA. Implement a model router for query handling.\nB. Create custom prompts.\nC. Implement Microsoft Power Platform connectors.\nD. Enable incremental indexing in Azure AI Search.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Implement a model router for query handling."},{"label":"B","text":"Create custom prompts."},{"label":"C","text":"Implement Microsoft Power Platform connectors."},{"label":"D","text":"Enable incremental indexing in Azure AI Search."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Implement Microsoft Power Platform connectors.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Implement Microsoft Power Platform connectors.\nThis option is correct because the customer data is distributed across multiple enterprise systems such as\nServiceNow, Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, and SharePoint Online.\nMicrosoft Power Platform connectors enable Microsoft Copilot Studio agents to securely connect to these\ndifferent data sources and retrieve real-time business information. By using connectors, the agent can\naccess updated records, customer details, tickets, inventory information, and documents from multiple\nplatforms without requiring manual data consolidation. This ensures that the agent provides accurate,\ncontext-aware, and timely responses to customer queries.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Implement a model router for query handling.\nThis is incorrect because model routing controls which AI model handles requests, but it does not provide\nconnectivity to enterprise data sources.\nB. Create custom prompts.\nThis is incorrect because prompts help guide AI behavior, but they do not establish access to external\nsystems or business data.\nD. Enable incremental indexing in Azure AI Search.\nThis is incorrect because Azure AI Search indexing is related to search optimization and indexing updates,\nnot direct integration across multiple enterprise applications.","sourcePages":[3,4],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":3,"stem":"A company has Microsoft Power Platform development, staging, and production environments. Each\nenvironment has its own Microsoft Dataverse tables and Azure AI Search index.\nYou are designing an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio\nagent between the environments.\nThe company has a Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 in development. Agent1 uses the following\ngrounding data sources:\n• A Dataverse table named CustomerOrders\n• An Azure AI Search index named customer-knowledge\nYou need to deploy Agent1 to production. The solution must ensure that the agent uses the production\ngrounding data sources, minimizes downtime, and handles credentials and endpoints securely.\nWhat should you include in the deployment package solution, and what should you reconfigure after the\ndeployment?","rawQuestion":"A company has Microsoft Power Platform development, staging, and production environments. Each\nenvironment has its own Microsoft Dataverse tables and Azure AI Search index.\nYou are designing an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio\nagent between the environments.\nThe company has a Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 in development. Agent1 uses the following\ngrounding data sources:\n• A Dataverse table named CustomerOrders\n• An Azure AI Search index named customer-knowledge\nYou need to deploy Agent1 to production. The solution must ensure that the agent uses the production\ngrounding data sources, minimizes downtime, and handles credentials and endpoints securely.\nWhat should you include in the deployment package solution, and what should you reconfigure after the\ndeployment?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Agent1 and references to the data sources\nThe environment variables","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nAgent1 and references to the data sources\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Power Platform ALM best practices recommend packaging the\nCopilot Studio agent together with references to the grounding data sources instead of embedding\nenvironment-specific configurations directly into the solution. By using references, the same solution can be\ndeployed across development, staging, and production environments while allowing each environment to\nconnect to its own Dataverse tables and Azure AI Search indexes. This approach improves portability,\nminimizes downtime during deployments, and supports standardized CI/CD processes.\nThe environment variables\nThis option is correct because environment variables are the preferred mechanism for storing and updating\nenvironment-specific settings such as Azure AI Search endpoints, index names, Dataverse configuration\nvalues, and service URLs. During deployment to production, the environment variables can be updated to\npoint Agent1 to the production Dataverse tables and the production Azure AI Search index without modifying\nthe solution itself. This aligns with Microsoft ALM and deployment automation best practices for secure and\nreusable deployments.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAgent1 only\nThis is incorrect because references to the grounding data sources must also be included.\nThe data sources only\nThis is incorrect because the Copilot Studio agent itself must be deployed.\nAgent1 and the data source connections\nThis is incorrect because environment-specific connections should not be tightly coupled to the deployment\npackage.\nAgent1, the data sources, and the data source connections\nThis is incorrect because embedding actual connections reduces portability and flexibility across\nenvironments.\nThe Dataverse connection only\nThis is incorrect because environment-specific settings extend beyond only the Dataverse connection.\nThe Azure AI Search connection only\nThis is incorrect because both Dataverse and Azure AI Search settings must be configurable.\nThe Dataverse and Azure AI Search connections\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft ALM best practices prefer reconfiguring environment variables rather\nthan manually modifying connections after deployment.\nThe Agent1 configuration\nThis is incorrect because the primary post-deployment change involves environment-specific configuration\nvalues.","sourcePages":[4,5,6],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":4,"stem":"You are creating validation criteria for a custom generative AI model that produces business reports\nbased on internal enterprise data.\nYou need to assess whether the model's outputs are appropriate and meaningful for the business reports.\nWhich metric should you use?","rawQuestion":"You are creating validation criteria for a custom generative AI model that produces business reports\nbased on internal enterprise data.\nYou need to assess whether the model's outputs are appropriate and meaningful for the business reports.\nWhich metric should you use?\nA. the number of active users interacting with the model\nB. alignment of the output to domain-specific tasks\nC. the average system resource usage during inference\nD. the model training duration","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"the number of active users interacting with the model"},{"label":"B","text":"alignment of the output to domain-specific tasks"},{"label":"C","text":"the average system resource usage during inference"},{"label":"D","text":"the model training duration"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. alignment of the output to domain-specific tasks","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. alignment of the output to domain-specific tasks\nThis option is correct because the primary requirement is to determine whether the generative AI model\nproduces business reports that are appropriate, meaningful, and relevant to the organization's needs.\nEvaluating alignment to domain-specific tasks measures how well the model understands business context,\nfollows reporting requirements, generates accurate insights, and produces outputs that are useful for\nenterprise decision-making. In generative AI validation, task alignment is a key quality metric because it\ndirectly evaluates whether the AI system fulfills the intended business purpose.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. the number of active users interacting with the model\nThis is incorrect because user activity measures adoption and usage, not output quality or business\nrelevance.\nC. the average system resource usage during inference\nThis is incorrect because resource usage measures performance efficiency, not whether the generated\nreports are meaningful.\nD. the model training duration\nThis is incorrect because training duration does not indicate the quality or appropriateness of the generated\noutputs.","sourcePages":[6,7],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":5,"stem":"A company has an Azure environment that supports multiple business units.\nThe company plans to implement an AI solution that will perform sentiment analysis on customer product\nreviews. You need to evaluate the potential cost of the solution to support return on AI investment (ROAI)\nanalysis.\nWhat should you use?","rawQuestion":"A company has an Azure environment that supports multiple business units.\nThe company plans to implement an AI solution that will perform sentiment analysis on customer product\nreviews. You need to evaluate the potential cost of the solution to support return on AI investment (ROAI)\nanalysis.\nWhat should you use?\nA. Cost Management + Billing\nB. Microsoft Fabric SKU Estimator\nC. Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator\nD. Azure Reservations","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Cost Management + Billing"},{"label":"B","text":"Microsoft Fabric SKU Estimator"},{"label":"C","text":"Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator"},{"label":"D","text":"Azure Reservations"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) Calculator\nThis option is correct because the Azure TCO Calculator is specifically designed to estimate and compare the\ncost of running workloads in Azure. In this scenario, the company wants to evaluate the potential cost of\nimplementing an AI solution to support Return on AI Investment (ROAI) analysis. The TCO Calculator helps\nestimate infrastructure and operational costs associated with Azure services and is commonly used during\nplanning and financial assessment phases before deployment. It provides cost projections that help\norganizations assess business value and investment feasibility.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Cost Management + Billing\nThis is incorrect because Cost Management + Billing is primarily used for monitoring, tracking, and\noptimizing existing Azure spending after resources are deployed.\nB. Microsoft Fabric SKU Estimator\nThis is incorrect because the Fabric SKU Estimator is intended for Microsoft Fabric capacity planning, not\nAzure AI solution cost estimation.\nD. Azure Reservations\nThis is incorrect because Azure Reservations are used to reduce costs through long-term committed usage\ndiscounts, not for initial ROAI cost evaluation.","sourcePages":[7],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":6,"stem":"You need to design a multi-agent solution that will include a custom agent. The solution must meet the\nfollowing requirements:\n• Define the rules and constraints that the agent must follow.\n• Automate a backend process that involves data movement between services and runs independently\nof the agent's reasoning steps.\nWhat should you include in the design for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"You need to design a multi-agent solution that will include a custom agent. The solution must meet the\nfollowing requirements:\n• Define the rules and constraints that the agent must follow.\n• Automate a backend process that involves data movement between services and runs independently\nof the agent's reasoning steps.\nWhat should you include in the design for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Conversation topics\nMicrosoft Power Automate cloud flow","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nConversation topics\nThis option is correct because conversation topics are used to define how the custom agent behaves during\ninteractions, including rules, constraints, response logic, branching conditions, and conversation guidance. In\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio, topics help control the agent's conversational boundaries and ensure that the agent\nfollows predefined business logic and governance requirements. They are commonly used to guide how the\nagent responds to user inputs and handles specific scenarios.\nMicrosoft Power Automate cloud flow\nThis option is correct because Power Automate cloud flows are designed to automate backend business\nprocesses, orchestrate integrations, move data between systems, and execute workflows independently of\nthe agent's reasoning process. Cloud flows are ideal for scenarios involving data movement, API integration,\nnotifications, approvals, and automation across multiple services. They operate separately from the\nconversational logic of the agent.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAgent flows\nThis is incorrect because agent flows are associated with orchestration and execution logic, not specifically\nfor defining conversational rules and constraints.\nConversation topics for backend automation\nThis is incorrect because topics manage conversational interactions rather than backend workflow\nautomation.\nMicrosoft Power Pages\nThis is incorrect because Power Pages is used for creating external-facing websites and portals, not backend\nprocess automation.","sourcePages":[8,9],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":7,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance to manage accounts payable. You are designing an AI\ninvoice processing solution.\nYou need to recommend the prerequisites to configure a prebuilt copilot for accounts payable. What should\nyou recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance to manage accounts payable. You are designing an AI\ninvoice processing solution.\nYou need to recommend the prerequisites to configure a prebuilt copilot for accounts payable. What should\nyou recommend?\nA. From Microsoft Copilot Studio, create an accounts payable agent.\nB. Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales to an accounts payable agent.\nC. Build an AI tool in Microsoft Foundry.\nD. From the Power Platform admin center, assign the Finance and Operations AI security role to users.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"From Microsoft Copilot Studio, create an accounts payable agent."},{"label":"B","text":"Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales to an accounts payable agent."},{"label":"C","text":"Build an AI tool in Microsoft Foundry."},{"label":"D","text":"From the Power Platform admin center, assign the Finance and Operations AI security role to users."}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. From the Power Platform admin center, assign the Finance and Operations AI security role to users.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. From the Power Platform admin center, assign the Finance and Operations AI security role to users.\nThis option is correct because prebuilt copilot capabilities for Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations\napplications require the appropriate AI-related security roles and permissions before users can access and\nconfigure the functionality. Assigning the Finance and Operations AI security role ensures that authorized\nusers can use AI-powered features such as accounts payable invoice processing and copilot experiences\nwithin Dynamics 365 Finance. Configuring the required permissions is a prerequisite for enabling and using\nthese built-in AI capabilities securely.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. From Microsoft Copilot Studio, create an accounts payable agent.\nThis is incorrect because the question refers to configuring a prebuilt copilot, not creating a custom copilot\nagent from scratch.\nB. Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales to an accounts payable agent.\nThis is incorrect because Copilot for Sales is intended for sales workflows and is unrelated to Dynamics 365\nFinance accounts payable processing.\nC. Build an AI tool in Microsoft Foundry.\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Foundry is not required to enable or configure the prebuilt accounts\npayable copilot functionality.","sourcePages":[9,10],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":8,"stem":"You need to design a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that meets the following requirements:\n• Supports interactive speech responses\n• Optimizes decision-making and the accuracy of responses\nWhat should you include in the design for each requirement? Each option may be used once, more than\nonce, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"You need to design a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that meets the following requirements:\n• Supports interactive speech responses\n• Optimizes decision-making and the accuracy of responses\nWhat should you include in the design for each requirement? Each option may be used once, more than\nonce, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Copilot Studio voice features\nA deep reasoning model","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nCopilot Studio voice features\nThis option is correct because Copilot Studio voice features provide the integrated voice capability required\nfor interactive speech experiences within a Copilot Studio agent. These features enable speech-to-text and\ntext-to-speech interactions, allowing users to communicate naturally with the agent through voice channels.\nIn a Copilot Studio design scenario, Microsoft expects architects to use the platform's built-in voice\ncapabilities rather than directly integrating lower-level speech infrastructure services.\nA deep reasoning model\nThis option is correct because deep reasoning models are optimized for complex decision-making, contextual\nunderstanding, multi-step reasoning, and improved response accuracy. These models can analyze user intent\nmore effectively, validate reasoning paths, and generate higher-quality responses. In enterprise AI solutions,\ndeep reasoning models are commonly used when the goal is to improve the reliability and intelligence of\nagent responses.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure Language in Foundry Tools\nThis is incorrect because it mainly provides language AI capabilities and does not directly optimize advanced\nreasoning or interactive voice functionality.\nAzure AI Speech\nThis is incorrect because Azure AI Speech is the underlying infrastructure service, while the question focuses\non the Copilot Studio design feature to include.\nSpeech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML)\nThis is incorrect because SSML is used to customize speech output formatting and pronunciation, not to\nprovide interactive speech capabilities.","sourcePages":[10,11],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":9,"stem":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels.\nYou need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance\ninsights for the agents.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels.\nYou need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance\ninsights for the agents.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Application Insights\nB. Azure Advisor\nC. Azure DevOps\nD. Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Application Insights"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure Advisor"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure DevOps"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Application Insights","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Application Insights\nThis option is correct because Application Insights provides comprehensive telemetry, monitoring,\ndiagnostics, and performance analytics for applications and AI solutions, including Microsoft Copilot Studio\nagents. It enables organizations to track request performance, response times, failures, usage patterns,\ndependencies, and user interactions across different channels. Application Insights also helps monitor agent\nhealth, troubleshoot issues, and analyze operational behavior using centralized telemetry and analytics\ndashboards. This makes it the most suitable solution for monitoring Copilot Studio agents at scale.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Azure Advisor\nThis is incorrect because Azure Advisor provides best-practice recommendations for Azure resources but\ndoes not deliver detailed telemetry or conversational performance insights for Copilot Studio agents.\nC. Azure DevOps\nThis is incorrect because Azure DevOps is used for software development lifecycle management, CI/CD, and\nproject tracking rather than runtime telemetry monitoring.\nD. Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice\nThis is incorrect because Customer Voice is used for surveys and feedback collection, not operational\ntelemetry or agent performance monitoring.","sourcePages":[11,12],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":10,"stem":"A company uses a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to automate tasks in a web app.\nDuring testing, you discover that the automation sometimes fails because of frequent changes to the app's\nuser interface.\nYou need to recommend a solution to ensure that the agent successfully automates the tasks. The solution\nmust minimize changes to the agent.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company uses a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to automate tasks in a web app.\nDuring testing, you discover that the automation sometimes fails because of frequent changes to the app's\nuser interface.\nYou need to recommend a solution to ensure that the agent successfully automates the tasks. The solution\nmust minimize changes to the agent.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Computer Use in Copilot Studio\nB. custom models in Azure AI Studio\nC. conversation topics in Copilot Studio\nD. an agent flow in Copilot Studio","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Computer Use in Copilot Studio"},{"label":"B","text":"custom models in Azure AI Studio"},{"label":"C","text":"conversation topics in Copilot Studio"},{"label":"D","text":"an agent flow in Copilot Studio"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Computer Use in Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Computer Use in Copilot Studio\nThis option is correct because Computer Use in Copilot Studio is specifically designed to help agents interact\nwith user interfaces dynamically, even when UI elements or layouts change frequently. It uses AI-driven\ninteraction capabilities to identify and operate applications in a more adaptive manner compared to rigid UI\nautomation approaches. This minimizes the need to constantly modify the agent whenever the web\napplication's interface changes. It is especially useful for resilient automation scenarios involving evolving\nweb apps and desktop interfaces.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. custom models in Azure AI Studio\nThis is incorrect because custom AI models are used for AI inference and model customization, not UI\nautomation resilience.\nC. conversation topics in Copilot Studio\nThis is incorrect because conversation topics manage conversational logic and interaction flows, not\nautomation of changing application interfaces.\nD. an agent flow in Copilot Studio\nThis is incorrect because agent flows orchestrate processes and workflows but do not specifically address UI\ninstability caused by interface changes.","sourcePages":[12,13],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":11,"stem":"You are evaluating a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that supports Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer\nService representatives.\nYou need to recommend a testing solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Evaluates agent effectiveness during active sessions\n• Validates whether the agent delivers accurate and helpful responses\n• Provides measurable, actionable insights for continuous improvement\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"You are evaluating a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that supports Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer\nService representatives.\nYou need to recommend a testing solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Evaluates agent effectiveness during active sessions\n• Validates whether the agent delivers accurate and helpful responses\n• Provides measurable, actionable insights for continuous improvement\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Track resolution, deflection, and accuracy by using dashboards and use scripts to ensure consistent\nresponses.\nB. Perform load testing to validate how the agent scales under a high chat volume.\nC. Review historical tickets to find agents that have the shortest resolution times.\nD. Measure uptime and page load times.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Track resolution, deflection, and accuracy by using dashboards and use scripts to ensure consistent responses."},{"label":"B","text":"Perform load testing to validate how the agent scales under a high chat volume."},{"label":"C","text":"Review historical tickets to find agents that have the shortest resolution times."},{"label":"D","text":"Measure uptime and page load times."}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Track resolution, deflection, and accuracy by using dashboards and use scripts to ensure consistent\nresponses.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Track resolution, deflection, and accuracy by using dashboards and use scripts to ensure consistent\nresponses.\nThis option is correct because the requirements focus on evaluating the effectiveness and quality of the\nCopilot Studio agent during real customer interactions. Tracking metrics such as resolution rate, deflection\nrate, and response accuracy directly measures how successfully the agent assists Customer Service\nrepresentatives and resolves customer issues. Dashboards provide measurable and actionable insights that\nhelp organizations continuously improve the agent's performance. Using scripts and standardized evaluation\nmethods also helps validate consistency and accuracy in the responses generated by the agent.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Perform load testing to validate how the agent scales under a high chat volume.\nThis is incorrect because load testing evaluates scalability and performance under stress, not response\nquality or effectiveness.\nC. Review historical tickets to find agents that have the shortest resolution times.\nThis is incorrect because it evaluates human support performance rather than validating the Copilot Studio\nagent's response quality and effectiveness.\nD. Measure uptime and page load times.\nThis is incorrect because uptime and page load metrics focus on infrastructure availability and performance,\nnot conversational accuracy or helpfulness.","sourcePages":[13,14],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":12,"stem":"You are designing end-to-end test scenarios for a business solution that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365\nSales and Dynamics 365 Finance.\nYou need to ensure that the business solution meets the following test requirements:\n• Properly exchanges data between the Dynamics 365 apps\n• Aligns with defined user workflows and business processes\nWhich type of testing should you use for each requirement? Each testing type may be used once, more than\nonce, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"You are designing end-to-end test scenarios for a business solution that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365\nSales and Dynamics 365 Finance.\nYou need to ensure that the business solution meets the following test requirements:\n• Properly exchanges data between the Dynamics 365 apps\n• Aligns with defined user workflows and business processes\nWhich type of testing should you use for each requirement? Each testing type may be used once, more than\nonce, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Integration\nUser acceptance","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nIntegration\nThis option is correct because integration testing validates that different systems, applications, and services\nexchange data correctly and work together as expected. In this scenario, the requirement focuses on\nverifying that data is properly exchanged between Dynamics 365 applications. Integration testing ensures\nthat interfaces, APIs, workflows, and connected systems communicate accurately without data loss or\nsynchronization issues.\nUser acceptance\nThis option is correct because user acceptance testing validates that the solution aligns with real business\nworkflows, operational requirements, and user expectations. It confirms that the implemented processes\nsupport the intended business scenarios and that end users can successfully perform their tasks using the\nsystem. User acceptance testing is commonly performed before production rollout to ensure business\nreadiness.\nIncorrect Answers:\nDrift\nThis is incorrect because drift testing is related to detecting changes in AI model behavior or data patterns\nover time.\nExploratory\nThis is incorrect because exploratory testing is informal testing used to discover unexpected issues rather\nthan validate defined business workflows.\nPerformance\nThis is incorrect because performance testing measures responsiveness, scalability, and system efficiency\nrather than validating workflow alignment or data exchange correctness.","sourcePages":[14,15],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":13,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Foundry agents.\nYou need to ensure that an agent can dynamically use external tools at runtime without updating the agent.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Foundry agents.\nYou need to ensure that an agent can dynamically use external tools at runtime without updating the agent.\nWhat should you include in the solution?\nA. a Microsoft Foundry hub\nB. a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server\nC. Azure AI Search\nD. Microsoft Copilot Studio","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"a Microsoft Foundry hub"},{"label":"B","text":"a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure AI Search"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Copilot Studio"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server\nThis option is correct because a Model Context Protocol, or MCP, server enables AI agents to dynamically\ndiscover and use external tools, APIs, and resources at runtime without requiring updates to the agent itself.\nMCP provides a standardized mechanism for connecting agents to external capabilities in a flexible and\nextensible manner. This allows new tools and integrations to be added independently while the agent\ndynamically accesses them as needed during execution. It is specifically designed to support tool\ninteroperability and runtime extensibility for AI agents.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. a Microsoft Foundry hub\nThis is incorrect because a Foundry hub is used for organizing and managing AI resources, not for\ndynamically exposing external tools at runtime.\nC. Azure AI Search\nThis is incorrect because Azure AI Search provides indexing and retrieval capabilities for data search\nscenarios, not dynamic tool integration.\nD. Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThis is incorrect because Copilot Studio is used for creating and managing conversational agents, but it does\nnot itself provide dynamic runtime tool discovery like MCP.","sourcePages":[15,16],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":14,"stem":"A company deploys agents that generate responses by using Azure OpenAI resources. The agents are\ndeployed to both the United States and Europe.\nYou need to recommend a governance solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Enforces the deployment of the resources to only approved Azure regions\n• Provides continuous compliance verification of the resources\nWhat should you include in the recommendation for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"A company deploys agents that generate responses by using Azure OpenAI resources. The agents are\ndeployed to both the United States and Europe.\nYou need to recommend a governance solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Enforces the deployment of the resources to only approved Azure regions\n• Provides continuous compliance verification of the resources\nWhat should you include in the recommendation for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Azure Policy\nMicrosoft Defender for Cloud","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nAzure Policy\nThis option is correct because Azure Policy is specifically designed to enforce governance and compliance\nrules across Azure environments. It can restrict resource deployments to approved Azure regions by using\npolicy definitions that deny deployments outside allowed geographic locations. This helps organizations\nmaintain regulatory compliance, data residency requirements, and governance standards across multi-region\nAzure deployments.\nMicrosoft Defender for Cloud\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Defender for Cloud provides continuous security posture\nassessment, compliance monitoring, and regulatory compliance verification for Azure resources. It\ncontinuously evaluates deployed resources against compliance standards and security recommendations,\nhelping organizations identify noncompliant resources and maintain ongoing governance visibility across\nenvironments.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure Monitor\nThis is incorrect because Azure Monitor focuses on telemetry, logging, and operational monitoring rather\nthan governance enforcement or compliance verification.\nMicrosoft Purview\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Purview is primarily used for data governance, data cataloging, and data\ncompliance management.\nMicrosoft Sentinel\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Sentinel is a SIEM and SOAR solution focused on security event\nmonitoring and threat detection.","sourcePages":[16,17],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":15,"stem":"You are designing a low-code AI business solution by using Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nThe solution must include an agent that automates tasks by simulating user interactions across third-party\napps and websites, such as clicking buttons, entering text, and extracting information from screens.\nYou need to recommend what to include in the agent. What should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"You are designing a low-code AI business solution by using Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nThe solution must include an agent that automates tasks by simulating user interactions across third-party\napps and websites, such as clicking buttons, entering text, and extracting information from screens.\nYou need to recommend what to include in the agent. What should you recommend?\nA. Model Context Protocol (MCP)\nB. a natural language understanding + (NLU+) model in Copilot Studio\nC. Computer Use in Copilot Studio\nD. Copilot skills","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)"},{"label":"B","text":"a natural language understanding + (NLU+) model in Copilot Studio"},{"label":"C","text":"Computer Use in Copilot Studio"},{"label":"D","text":"Copilot skills"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Computer Use in Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Computer Use in Copilot Studio\nThis option is correct because Computer Use in Copilot Studio is specifically designed to automate user\ninterface interactions across applications and websites. It enables agents to simulate human actions such as\nclicking buttons, entering text, navigating screens, and extracting information from interfaces. This capability\nis particularly useful when integrating with third-party applications that may not expose APIs. Computer Use\nallows low-code AI agents to interact dynamically with graphical interfaces and automate tasks in a human-\nlike manner.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Model Context Protocol (MCP)\nThis is incorrect because MCP enables dynamic access to external tools and services, not UI-based\ninteraction automation.\nB. a natural language understanding + (NLU+) model in Copilot Studio\nThis is incorrect because NLU+ models are used for intent recognition and language understanding rather\nthan interface automation.\nD. Copilot skills\nThis is incorrect because Copilot skills extend functionality and task capabilities but do not specifically\nprovide simulated UI interaction across apps and websites.","sourcePages":[18],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":16,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform solution that contains the following components:\n• Microsoft Dataverse tables\n• A Microsoft Power BI workspace named WS1\n• A canvas app named App1 that uses Dataverse\n• A Power BI semantic model that connects to Dataverse by using DirectQuery\nYou plan to use generative AI to provide answers to queries based on a subset of corporate data. You need to\nensure that the data is available as a grounding data source for AI systems.\nWhat should you do?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform solution that contains the following components:\n• Microsoft Dataverse tables\n• A Microsoft Power BI workspace named WS1\n• A canvas app named App1 that uses Dataverse\n• A Power BI semantic model that connects to Dataverse by using DirectQuery\nYou plan to use generative AI to provide answers to queries based on a subset of corporate data. You need to\nensure that the data is available as a grounding data source for AI systems.\nWhat should you do?\nA. Populate a Dataverse table.\nB. Share WS1.\nC. Endorse the semantic model.\nD. Export the semantic model.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Populate a Dataverse table."},{"label":"B","text":"Share WS1."},{"label":"C","text":"Endorse the semantic model."},{"label":"D","text":"Export the semantic model."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Endorse the semantic model.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Endorse the semantic model.\nThis option is correct because endorsed Power BI semantic models are recognized as trusted and certified\ndata sources that can be used as grounding data for generative AI experiences within Microsoft AI and Power\nPlatform ecosystems. Endorsement improves discoverability, trust, governance, and usability of the semantic\nmodel for AI-based querying and reasoning scenarios. Since the requirement is to make a subset of\ncorporate data available as a grounding data source for AI systems, endorsing the semantic model is the\nappropriate governance and AI-readiness step.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Populate a Dataverse table.\nThis is incorrect because the data already exists and the requirement is specifically about making it available\nas a trusted grounding source for AI systems.\nB. Share WS1.\nThis is incorrect because sharing a Power BI workspace grants access permissions but does not establish\nthe semantic model as a trusted grounding data source.\nD. Export the semantic model.\nThis is incorrect because exporting the semantic model does not help AI systems discover or use it as a\ngoverned grounding source.","sourcePages":[18,19],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":17,"stem":"A company plans to implement an AI solution that will contain a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent and a\nMicrosoft Foundry agent. The solution will be stored in a source code repository.\nYou need to recommend a deployment method for each agent. The solution must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• A test environment must be used before a deployment to production.\n• Production must be isolated from development and testing.\n• The deployment must be repeatable and fully automated.\n• The solution must NOT require manual intervention.\n•\nWhich deployment method should you recommend for each agent?","rawQuestion":"A company plans to implement an AI solution that will contain a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent and a\nMicrosoft Foundry agent. The solution will be stored in a source code repository.\nYou need to recommend a deployment method for each agent. The solution must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• A test environment must be used before a deployment to production.\n• Production must be isolated from development and testing.\n• The deployment must be repeatable and fully automated.\n• The solution must NOT require manual intervention.\n•\nWhich deployment method should you recommend for each agent?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Use a Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipeline.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nUse a Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipeline.\nThis option is correct for Copilot Studio because Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipelines are\nspecifically designed to automate application lifecycle management across development, test, and production\nenvironments within the Power Platform ecosystem. Deployment pipelines support environment isolation,\nrepeatable deployments, automated promotion between stages, and reduced manual intervention. They are\nthe recommended deployment approach for Copilot Studio solutions and Power Platform components.\nUse an Azure DevOps pipeline.\nThis option is correct for Microsoft Foundry because Foundry solutions and Azure-based AI resources are\ncommonly deployed using infrastructure-as-code and CI/CD automation through Azure DevOps pipelines.\nAzure DevOps supports fully automated deployments, testing stages, source control integration, environment\nseparation, and repeatable deployment processes. This aligns with the requirement for automation,\nproduction isolation, and deployment consistency.\nIncorrect Answers:\nExport from the source code repository and import to the target environment.\nThis is incorrect because it introduces manual intervention and does not provide a fully automated\ndeployment process.\nUse a Bicep file.\nThis is incorrect because Bicep is primarily an infrastructure-as-code deployment template and not a\ncomplete deployment orchestration solution for Copilot Studio ALM workflows.\nUse a Microsoft Power Platform deployment pipeline for Microsoft Foundry\nThis is incorrect because Power Platform deployment pipelines are intended for Power Platform solutions\nrather than Azure AI Foundry deployments.\nNote: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each\nquestion presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the\nstated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the\nsolutions in the set solve the problem.\nIn real exam, after you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these\nquestions do not appear on the Review Screen in exam.","sourcePages":[19,20,21],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":18,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.\nYou need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in\nMicrosoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship\nmanagement (CRM) data.\nSolution: You recommend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales.\nDoes this meet the goal?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.\nYou need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in\nMicrosoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship\nmanagement (CRM) data.\nSolution: You recommend Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales.\nDoes this meet the goal?\nA. Yes\nB. No","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Yes","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nA. Yes\nThis option is correct because Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales is designed to integrate Microsoft 365\napplications such as Outlook and Teams with Dynamics 365 CRM data. It can automatically summarize email\nthreads, generate AI-based suggested replies in Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries\nenriched with customer relationship management information from Dynamics 365. This directly satisfies all\nthe requirements stated in the scenario.","sourcePages":[21],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":19,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.\nYou need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in\nMicrosoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship\nmanagement (CRM) data.\nSolution: You recommend a classic Microsoft Dataverse workflow.\nDoes this meet the goal?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.\nYou need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in\nMicrosoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship\nmanagement (CRM) data.\nSolution: You recommend a classic Microsoft Dataverse workflow.\nDoes this meet the goal?\nA. Yes\nB. No","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. No","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. No\nSolution does not meet the goal because a classic Microsoft Dataverse workflow is a traditional business\nprocess automation mechanism and does not provide generative AI capabilities such as summarizing email\nthreads, generating suggested replies in Outlook, or creating AI-driven meeting preparation summaries with\nCRM context. These capabilities require Microsoft 365 Copilot for Sales or similar AI-powered Copilot\nservices that integrate Microsoft 365 productivity tools with Dynamics 365 CRM data.","sourcePages":[21,22],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":20,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.\nYou need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in\nMicrosoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship\nmanagement (CRM) data.\nSolution: You recommend a Microsoft 365 Copilot agent template.\nDoes this meet the goal?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft 365 and Dynamics 365.\nYou need to recommend a solution to automatically summarize email threads, generate suggested replies in\nMicrosoft Outlook, and provide meeting preparation summaries that include relevant customer relationship\nmanagement (CRM) data.\nSolution: You recommend a Microsoft 365 Copilot agent template.\nDoes this meet the goal?\nA. Yes\nB. No","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. No","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. No\nSolution does not meet the goal because a Microsoft 365 Copilot agent template alone does not specifically\nprovide the integrated sales productivity capabilities required in the scenario. The requirements include\nautomatic email summarization, suggested Outlook replies, and meeting preparation summaries enriched\nwith Dynamics 365 CRM data. These capabilities are specifically delivered through Microsoft 365 Copilot for\nSales, which is designed to integrate Microsoft 365 applications with Dynamics 365 customer relationship\nmanagement data.\nA generic Microsoft 365 Copilot agent template does not inherently provide these specialized CRM-integrated\nsales assistance features.","sourcePages":[22],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":21,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft 365 E5 subscription and uses Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nThe company has a Microsoft SharePoint Online library that contains 10,000 policy PDFs from various\ndepartments. The library contains a populated column named Department for each PDF.\nYou need to design a Copilot Studio agent that will use the SharePoint library as a knowledge source.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Enable the agent to answer user questions about company policies.\n• Ensure that the agent can identify which departments and policies are connected.\nWhat should you include in the design for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft 365 E5 subscription and uses Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nThe company has a Microsoft SharePoint Online library that contains 10,000 policy PDFs from various\ndepartments. The library contains a populated column named Department for each PDF.\nYou need to design a Copilot Studio agent that will use the SharePoint library as a knowledge source.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Enable the agent to answer user questions about company policies.\n• Ensure that the agent can identify which departments and policies are connected.\nWhat should you include in the design for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"From Copilot Studio, add SharePoint as a knowledge source.\nFrom Copilot Studio, configure the SharePoint tool.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nFrom Copilot Studio, add SharePoint as a knowledge source.\nThis option is correct because Copilot Studio supports SharePoint Online libraries as native knowledge\nsources. By adding the SharePoint library as a knowledge source, the agent can retrieve and use information\nfrom the policy PDFs to answer user questions. This enables retrieval-augmented responses directly from\nenterprise documents stored in SharePoint without requiring custom AI model development or manual data\nmigration.\nFrom Copilot Studio, configure the SharePoint tool.\nThis option is correct because configuring the SharePoint tool allows the agent to use SharePoint metadata\nand document context, including the populated Department column associated with each policy PDF. This\nhelps the agent identify relationships between departments and policies and provide context-aware\nresponses based on SharePoint document metadata.\nIncorrect Answers:\nBuild a custom model in Microsoft Foundry.\nThis is incorrect because Copilot Studio can directly use SharePoint as a knowledge source without requiring\na custom AI model.\nImport the PDFs into Microsoft Dataverse.\nThis is incorrect because the documents already exist in SharePoint Online and do not need to be migrated\nto Dataverse.\nUse AI Builder to process and feed SharePoint content.\nThis is incorrect because AI Builder is unnecessary when Copilot Studio natively supports SharePoint\nknowledge integration.\nApply Microsoft Purview sensitivity labels.\nThis is incorrect because sensitivity labels help classify and protect content but do not identify relationships\nbetween departments and policies.\nCreate a Microsoft Dataverse table for the departments.\nThis is incorrect because the Department metadata already exists within the SharePoint library.\nUpgrade to SharePoint Premium.\nThis is incorrect because SharePoint Premium is not required for using SharePoint Online as a Copilot Studio\nknowledge source.","sourcePages":[22,23,24],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":22,"stem":"A financial services company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance.\nCurrently, the company's support staff manually reviews customer transaction histories to detect potential\nfraud cases before escalating the cases.\nYou need to recommend an automation solution for the review process. The solution must ensure that\nescalations reach a human analyst for final decision making. What should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A financial services company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance.\nCurrently, the company's support staff manually reviews customer transaction histories to detect potential\nfraud cases before escalating the cases.\nYou need to recommend an automation solution for the review process. The solution must ensure that\nescalations reach a human analyst for final decision making. What should you recommend?\nA. Deploy an autonomous agent that closes non-fraud cases automatically.\nB. Use Microsoft 365 Copilot in Word to automatically finalize fraud detection policies.\nC. Configure a task agent to generate fraud risk scores for the human analyst to review.\nD. Export the data to a data lake for analysis in Microsoft Power BI.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Deploy an autonomous agent that closes non-fraud cases automatically."},{"label":"B","text":"Use Microsoft 365 Copilot in Word to automatically finalize fraud detection policies."},{"label":"C","text":"Configure a task agent to generate fraud risk scores for the human analyst to review."},{"label":"D","text":"Export the data to a data lake for analysis in Microsoft Power BI."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Configure a task agent to generate fraud risk scores for the human analyst to review.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Configure a task agent to generate fraud risk scores for the human analyst to review.\nThis option is correct because the requirement is to automate the fraud review process while ensuring that a\nhuman analyst makes the final decision. A task agent can analyze transaction histories, identify suspicious\npatterns, and generate fraud risk scores automatically. The results can then be escalated to a human analyst\nfor review and approval. This approach supports human-in-the-loop decision making, which is especially\nimportant in financial services scenarios involving compliance, risk management, and sensitive customer\ntransactions.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Deploy an autonomous agent that closes non-fraud cases automatically.\nThis is incorrect because the requirement specifically states that escalations must reach a human analyst for\nfinal decision making.\nB. Use Microsoft 365 Copilot in Word to automatically finalize fraud detection policies.\nThis is incorrect because Copilot in Word is unrelated to transaction fraud review automation.\nD. Export the data to a data lake for analysis in Microsoft Power BI.\nThis is incorrect because Power BI supports reporting and analytics but does not automate fraud review and\nescalation workflows.","sourcePages":[24,25],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":23,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that provides answers based on a knowledge base for\ncustomer support.\nUsers report that, occasionally, the agent provides inaccurate answers.\nYou need to use metrics from the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio to identify the cause of the inaccuracies.\nWhich two options should you use?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that provides answers based on a knowledge base for\ncustomer support.\nUsers report that, occasionally, the agent provides inaccurate answers.\nYou need to use metrics from the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio to identify the cause of the inaccuracies.\nWhich two options should you use?\nA. survey results\nB. session information and session outcomes\nC. topic usage and topics with low resolution\nD. engagement, resolution, and escalation rates\nE. quality of generated answers","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"survey results"},{"label":"B","text":"session information and session outcomes"},{"label":"C","text":"topic usage and topics with low resolution"},{"label":"D","text":"engagement, resolution, and escalation rates"},{"label":"E","text":"quality of generated answers"}],"correct":["C","E"],"answer":"C. topic usage and topics with low resolution\nE. quality of generated answers","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. topic usage and topics with low resolution\nThis option is correct because topic usage analytics help identify which topics are most frequently triggered\nand which topics fail to resolve customer queries successfully. Topics with low resolution rates indicate that\nusers are not receiving satisfactory answers or that the conversation flow is not properly addressing their\nrequests. Analyzing low-resolution topics helps identify where inaccuracies or gaps in the agent's logic and\nresponse handling are occurring.\nE. quality of generated answers\nThis option is correct because the quality of generated answers metric directly evaluates the relevance,\ngrounding quality, and usefulness of AI-generated responses from the knowledge base. Since the agent\ngenerates answers using enterprise knowledge sources, this metric is critical for identifying whether\ninaccurate responses are caused by poor grounding, outdated knowledge content, or issues in generative\nanswer quality. It provides targeted insights into the effectiveness of generated AI responses.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. survey results\nThis is incorrect because survey results provide user satisfaction feedback but do not directly identify the\nroot cause of inaccurate answers.\nB. session information and session outcomes\nThis is incorrect because session metrics provide high-level operational insights rather than directly\ndiagnosing answer accuracy issues.\nD. engagement, resolution, and escalation rates\nThis is incorrect because these metrics measure overall operational performance and user interaction trends\nrather than the quality of generated answers.","sourcePages":[25,26],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":24,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance for accounts payable and customer debt recovery.\nYou are designing an AI finance process that meets the following requirements:\n• Provides AI-driven details to help staff identify overdue vendor invoices and outstanding balances\n• Helps staff reduce how long it takes to review overdue invoices and payment history\nYou need to recommend which Microsoft Copilot features to include in the design.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance for accounts payable and customer debt recovery.\nYou are designing an AI finance process that meets the following requirements:\n• Provides AI-driven details to help staff identify overdue vendor invoices and outstanding balances\n• Helps staff reduce how long it takes to review overdue invoices and payment history\nYou need to recommend which Microsoft Copilot features to include in the design.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"The Account Reconciliation agent\nCollections coordinator summary","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nThe Account Reconciliation agent\nThis option is correct because the Account Reconciliation agent is specifically designed to support\nreconciliation and financial matching processes in Dynamics 365 Finance. It helps identify overdue vendor\ninvoices, outstanding balances, discrepancies, and unpaid financial items across accounts payable\noperations. The agent provides AI-driven visibility into reconciliation issues and helps staff quickly identify\naccounts that require attention, making it the most appropriate solution for overdue invoice and balance\nidentification.\nCollections coordinator summary\nThis option is correct because the Collections coordinator summary uses AI-generated summaries to\nconsolidate overdue invoice details, payment history, customer balances, and collections information into an\neasily reviewable format. This significantly reduces the manual effort and time required for staff to analyze\ncustomer debt recovery information and payment activities. It improves productivity by presenting key\ninsights and overdue account details in a summarized view.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAgent management\nThis is incorrect because it is used for managing AI agents rather than finance-specific reconciliation or\ncollections activities.\nAI Summaries with Copilot\nThis is incorrect because it is a general summarization capability and is less targeted than the specialized\ncollections functionality.\nThe Supplier Communication agent\nThis is incorrect because it focuses on supplier communication workflows rather than identifying overdue\nbalances and reconciliation issues.\nAnalyze demand plans with Copilot\nThis is incorrect because it is related to supply chain forecasting and demand planning rather than finance\noperations.","sourcePages":[26,27],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":25,"stem":"A company uses a fine-tuned Microsoft Foundry model that requires frequent updates as new customer\nfeedback becomes available.\nYou need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process that meets the following\nrequirements:\n• Data changes must be tracked and versioned.\n• The model must be retrained consistently by using approved training data.\nWhich two actions should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A company uses a fine-tuned Microsoft Foundry model that requires frequent updates as new customer\nfeedback becomes available.\nYou need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process that meets the following\nrequirements:\n• Data changes must be tracked and versioned.\n• The model must be retrained consistently by using approved training data.\nWhich two actions should you include in the design?\nA. Associate the storage location to the fine-tuning job.\nB. Create a content filter.\nC. Store the training data in Azure Files.\nD. Upload the training data to Microsoft Foundry data files\nE. Store the training data in Azure Blob Storage that has version control enabled.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Associate the storage location to the fine-tuning job."},{"label":"B","text":"Create a content filter."},{"label":"C","text":"Store the training data in Azure Files."},{"label":"D","text":"Upload the training data to Microsoft Foundry data files"},{"label":"E","text":"Store the training data in Azure Blob Storage that has version control enabled."}],"correct":["D","E"],"answer":"D. Upload the training data to Microsoft Foundry data files\nE. Store the training data in Azure Blob Storage that has version control enabled.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Upload the training data to Microsoft Foundry data files\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Foundry data files provide managed and governed data assets for AI\ntraining workflows. By uploading approved training datasets into Foundry data files, organizations can\nstandardize which datasets are used during fine-tuning and retraining operations. This helps ensure\nrepeatable and consistent model retraining processes while maintaining governance and traceability within\nthe AI lifecycle management process.\nE. Store the training data in Azure Blob Storage that has version control enabled.\nThis option is correct because Azure Blob Storage with version control enabled supports tracking and\nversioning of training dataset changes over time. Since the requirement explicitly states that data changes\nmust be tracked and versioned, Blob versioning provides the necessary governance capabilities, including\nhistorical dataset tracking, rollback support, auditability, and controlled dataset evolution. This is a best\npractice for enterprise AI ALM processes.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Associate the storage location to the fine-tuning job.\nThis is incorrect because associating storage to a fine-tuning job is primarily an operational configuration\nstep rather than a core ALM strategy for governed dataset management.\nB. Create a content filter.\nThis is incorrect because content filters are used for AI safety and moderation during inference, not for\ndataset lifecycle management or retraining governance.\nC. Store the training data in Azure Files.\nThis is incorrect because Azure Files is intended for shared file access scenarios and does not provide the\nsame AI-focused versioning and ALM capabilities as Blob Storage.","sourcePages":[28],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":26,"stem":"You are designing two Microsoft Copilot Studio agents named Agent1 and Agent2.\nEach agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Each agent must use a standard model.\n• Each agent must NOT use generative orchestration.\n• Agent1 must support simple and short phrases for a given topic.\n• Agent2 must integrate with Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center voice channel.\nYou need to recommend language models for the agents.\nWhat should you recommend for each agent? Each language model may be used once, more than once, or\nnot at all.","rawQuestion":"You are designing two Microsoft Copilot Studio agents named Agent1 and Agent2.\nEach agent must meet the following requirements:\n• Each agent must use a standard model.\n• Each agent must NOT use generative orchestration.\n• Agent1 must support simple and short phrases for a given topic.\n• Agent2 must integrate with Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center voice channel.\nYou need to recommend language models for the agents.\nWhat should you recommend for each agent? Each language model may be used once, more than once, or\nnot at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Natural language understanding (NLU) for Agent1\nNatural language understanding + (NLU+) for Agent2","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nNatural language understanding (NLU) for Agent1\nThis option is correct because Agent1 must support simple and short phrases for a given topic while using a\nstandard model without generative orchestration. Traditional NLU in Copilot Studio is optimized for intent\nrecognition and deterministic topic routing based on predefined trigger phrases. It works well for structured\nconversational scenarios involving short and predictable user inputs.\nNatural language understanding + (NLU+) for Agent2\nThis option is correct because Agent2 must integrate with the Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center voice\nchannel while still using a standard non-generative model. NLU+ is designed for advanced conversational and\nvoice scenarios such as IVR and contact center integrations. It supports more sophisticated spoken-language\nunderstanding, enhanced entity extraction, and improved handling of natural speech while remaining within\nclassic orchestration models rather than generative orchestration.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure Language in Foundry Tools\nThis is incorrect because it is a broader Azure AI capability and not the intended Copilot Studio language\nmodel choice for this scenario.\nAzure OpenAI\nThis is incorrect because Azure OpenAI is associated with generative AI capabilities, while the requirement\nexplicitly states that generative orchestration must not be used.\nConversational language understanding (CLU)\nThis is incorrect because, although CLU supports conversational AI scenarios, Microsoft Copilot Studio\nstandard orchestration for Dynamics 365 Contact Center voice scenarios is more specifically aligned with\nNLU+.","sourcePages":[29,30],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":27,"stem":"A company has two Microsoft Power Platform environments named Dev1 and Prod1.\nA Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 is built into a solution in the Dev1 environment.\nYou plan to deploy Agent1 to Prod1.\nYou need to make Agent1 available to the users in Prod1.\nThe solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you do?","rawQuestion":"A company has two Microsoft Power Platform environments named Dev1 and Prod1.\nA Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 is built into a solution in the Dev1 environment.\nYou plan to deploy Agent1 to Prod1.\nYou need to make Agent1 available to the users in Prod1.\nThe solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you do?\nA. Share Agent1 with the users in Prod1.\nB. Export the solution as an unmanaged solution and import the solution into Prod1.\nC. Export the solution as a managed solution and import the solution into Prod1.\nD. Create a new Copilot Studio agent in Prod1 by replicating the configuration of Agent1.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Share Agent1 with the users in Prod1."},{"label":"B","text":"Export the solution as an unmanaged solution and import the solution into Prod1."},{"label":"C","text":"Export the solution as a managed solution and import the solution into Prod1."},{"label":"D","text":"Create a new Copilot Studio agent in Prod1 by replicating the configuration of Agent1."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Export the solution as a managed solution and import the solution into Prod1.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Export the solution as a managed solution and import the solution into Prod1.\nThis option is correct because managed solutions are the recommended deployment method for moving\nMicrosoft Power Platform and Copilot Studio solutions from development to production environments.\nManaged solutions provide controlled deployment, protect production customizations, support application\nlifecycle management best practices, and minimize administrative effort. They are specifically intended for\nproduction deployments where users consume the solution rather than modify it.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Share Agent1 with the users in Prod1.\nThis is incorrect because sharing an agent does not deploy the solution or make it available within a separate\nPower Platform environment.\nB. Export the solution as an unmanaged solution and import the solution into Prod1.\nThis is incorrect because unmanaged solutions are typically used for development environments and can\nlead to uncontrolled modifications in production.\nD. Create a new Copilot Studio agent in Prod1 by replicating the configuration of Agent1.\nThis is incorrect because manually recreating the agent increases administrative effort and does not follow\nproper ALM practices.","sourcePages":[30,31],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":28,"stem":"You are designing an AI strategy for Microsoft Dynamics 365 finance and operations apps.\nYou are evaluating the use of Microsoft Copilot Studio to provide in-app help and guidance based on\ngenerative AI general knowledge. You need to recommend which knowledge sources to include in the\ngenerative help and guidance agent. The solution must minimize the risk of generating inaccurate responses.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"You are designing an AI strategy for Microsoft Dynamics 365 finance and operations apps.\nYou are evaluating the use of Microsoft Copilot Studio to provide in-app help and guidance based on\ngenerative AI general knowledge. You need to recommend which knowledge sources to include in the\ngenerative help and guidance agent. The solution must minimize the risk of generating inaccurate responses.\nWhat should you recommend?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Must be uploaded to the agent\nMust be disabled for the agent","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMust be uploaded to the agent\nThis option is correct because using curated and approved custom knowledge sources helps ground the\nCopilot Studio agent on trusted enterprise content related to Dynamics 365 finance and operations\nprocesses. Uploading controlled knowledge sources improves response accuracy and reduces the likelihood\nof hallucinations or irrelevant answers. Grounding the agent on authoritative business documentation is a\nbest practice for enterprise AI guidance solutions.\nMust be disabled for the agent\nThis option is correct because enabling AI general knowledge allows the agent to answer using broad\ninternet-scale or generalized AI knowledge, which increases the risk of inaccurate, noncompliant, or\nhallucinated responses. Since the requirement explicitly states that the solution must minimize the risk of\ngenerating inaccurate responses, disabling AI general knowledge ensures that answers are generated only\nfrom approved enterprise knowledge sources.\nIncorrect Answers:\nMust be excluded from the agent\nThis is incorrect because excluding custom knowledge sources would remove the trusted enterprise\ngrounding required for accurate responses.\nAre not supported\nThis is incorrect because custom knowledge sources are fully supported in Copilot Studio.\nMust be enabled for the agent\nThis is incorrect because enabling AI general knowledge increases the likelihood of inaccurate or ungrounded\nresponses.\nIs not supported\nThis is incorrect because AI general knowledge is supported in Copilot Studio.","sourcePages":[31,32],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":29,"stem":"A company has Microsoft 365 Copilot agents.\nYou need to design a security solution for the agents.\n• The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Identify and mitigate potential risks that relate to AI use.\n• Protect AI apps and the sensitive data processed or generated by the agents.\n• Support responsible AI governance by retaining and logging interactions, detecting policy violations,\nand investigating incidents.\nWhich two components should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A company has Microsoft 365 Copilot agents.\nYou need to design a security solution for the agents.\n• The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Identify and mitigate potential risks that relate to AI use.\n• Protect AI apps and the sensitive data processed or generated by the agents.\n• Support responsible AI governance by retaining and logging interactions, detecting policy violations,\nand investigating incidents.\nWhich two components should you include in the design?\nA. Microsoft Purview\nB. Azure AI Content Safety\nC. role-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry\nD. Microsoft Defender","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Purview"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure AI Content Safety"},{"label":"C","text":"role-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Defender"}],"correct":["A","D"],"answer":"A. Microsoft Purview\nD. Microsoft Defender","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Microsoft Purview\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Purview provides responsible AI governance, compliance\nmanagement, auditing, retention, eDiscovery, and policy enforcement capabilities for Microsoft 365 Copilot\nagents. It supports logging and retaining AI interactions, detecting policy violations, and investigating\nincidents involving sensitive enterprise data. Purview is a core governance and compliance solution for\nenterprise AI environments.\nD. Microsoft Defender\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Defender helps identify and mitigate AI-related security risks while\nprotecting AI applications and sensitive data processed or generated by the agents. Microsoft Defender\nprovides threat detection, security monitoring, anomaly detection, and protection capabilities that help secure\nAI-powered workloads and applications across the Microsoft ecosystem.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Azure AI Content Safety\nThis is incorrect because Azure AI Content Safety primarily focuses on harmful content detection and\nmoderation rather than comprehensive AI governance, security monitoring, and enterprise data protection.\nC. role-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry\nThis is incorrect because RBAC controls access permissions but does not provide full AI governance, policy\nviolation detection, logging, retention, or security monitoring capabilities.","sourcePages":[32,33],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":30,"stem":"A retail company plans to deploy Microsoft Copilot Studio agents to support:\n• Microsoft Dynamics 365 Commerce scenarios.\n• A Microsoft Power Apps inventory management solution.\nYou need to recommend a solution to organize product catalog data as a consistent source for multiple AI\nsystems.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A retail company plans to deploy Microsoft Copilot Studio agents to support:\n• Microsoft Dynamics 365 Commerce scenarios.\n• A Microsoft Power Apps inventory management solution.\nYou need to recommend a solution to organize product catalog data as a consistent source for multiple AI\nsystems.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Let each agent scrape product details from Microsoft SharePoint Online libraries.\nB. Store the product catalog data in a separate custom table for each agent.\nC. Configure prompts to pull product details from the PDFs of external vendors.\nD. Centralize the product catalog data in Microsoft Dataverse and expose the data to both agents.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Let each agent scrape product details from Microsoft SharePoint Online libraries."},{"label":"B","text":"Store the product catalog data in a separate custom table for each agent."},{"label":"C","text":"Configure prompts to pull product details from the PDFs of external vendors."},{"label":"D","text":"Centralize the product catalog data in Microsoft Dataverse and expose the data to both agents."}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Centralize the product catalog data in Microsoft Dataverse and expose the data to both agents.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Centralize the product catalog data in Microsoft Dataverse and expose the data to both agents.\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Dataverse provides a centralized, governed, scalable, and reusable\nenterprise data platform for Power Platform and Dynamics 365 solutions. By storing the product catalog in\nDataverse, both the Dynamics 365 Commerce agent and the Power Apps inventory management agent can\naccess a single consistent source of truth. This approach improves data consistency, reduces duplication,\nsimplifies governance, and enables multiple AI systems to use the same trusted business data for grounding\nand operational processes.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Let each agent scrape product details from Microsoft SharePoint Online libraries.\nThis is incorrect because scraping SharePoint content creates inconsistent and ungoverned data access\npatterns and is not a reliable enterprise data architecture.\nB. Store the product catalog data in a separate custom table for each agent.\nThis is incorrect because duplicating product data across separate tables increases maintenance overhead\nand creates data inconsistency risks.\nC. Configure prompts to pull product details from the PDFs of external vendors.\nThis is incorrect because relying on vendor PDFs is inefficient, difficult to govern, and does not provide a\ncentralized structured enterprise data source.","sourcePages":[33,34],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":31,"stem":"You need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process for a Microsoft Power Platform\nenvironment that contains a solution named Solution1. Solution1 must include a custom connector for\nCopilot in Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service.\nSolution1 must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the custom connector can be deployed consistently across environments as part of the\nALM process.\n• Allow the custom connector to be edited only in the development environment.\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"You need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process for a Microsoft Power Platform\nenvironment that contains a solution named Solution1. Solution1 must include a custom connector for\nCopilot in Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service.\nSolution1 must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the custom connector can be deployed consistently across environments as part of the\nALM process.\n• Allow the custom connector to be edited only in the development environment.\nWhat should you include in the design?\nA. Add the custom connector to GitHub.\nB. Share the custom connector.\nC. Create the custom connector in the default solution.\nD. Add the custom connector to Solution1.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Add the custom connector to GitHub."},{"label":"B","text":"Share the custom connector."},{"label":"C","text":"Create the custom connector in the default solution."},{"label":"D","text":"Add the custom connector to Solution1."}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Add the custom connector to Solution1.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Add the custom connector to Solution1.\nThis option is correct because custom connectors must be included inside a Power Platform solution to\nparticipate properly in application lifecycle management processes. By adding the custom connector to\nSolution1, the connector can be packaged, versioned, exported, and deployed consistently across\ndevelopment, test, and production environments. This also supports managed solution deployments,\nensuring that the connector remains editable only in the development environment while being protected in\ndownstream environments such as staging or production.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Add the custom connector to GitHub.\nThis is incorrect because GitHub can support source control, but it does not itself make the connector\ndeployable as part of Power Platform ALM processes.\nB. Share the custom connector.\nThis is incorrect because sharing provides access permissions but does not support controlled deployment\nor ALM lifecycle management.\nC. Create the custom connector in the default solution.\nThis is incorrect because components created in the default solution are not properly isolated for enterprise\nALM and deployment management.","sourcePages":[34,35],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":32,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses custom connectors to interact with\nenterprise APIs. You need to recommend an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure that\nthe connectors are deployed consistently across development, test, and production environments and meet\ngovernance and traceability requirements.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses custom connectors to interact with\nenterprise APIs. You need to recommend an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure that\nthe connectors are deployed consistently across development, test, and production environments and meet\ngovernance and traceability requirements.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Deploy the APIs as Azure Functions.\nB. Manage the connectors as solution components and deploy the components by using ALM pipelines.\nC. Maintain connector definitions in environment variables.\nD. Export and import the connectors between the environments as unmanaged solutions","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Deploy the APIs as Azure Functions."},{"label":"B","text":"Manage the connectors as solution components and deploy the components by using ALM pipelines."},{"label":"C","text":"Maintain connector definitions in environment variables."},{"label":"D","text":"Export and import the connectors between the environments as unmanaged solutions"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Manage the connectors as solution components and deploy the components by using ALM pipelines.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Manage the connectors as solution components and deploy the components by using ALM pipelines.\nThis option is correct because Power Platform custom connectors should be managed as solution\ncomponents to support proper application lifecycle management practices. Including connectors within\nsolutions enables versioning, governance, traceability, repeatable deployments, and controlled promotion\nacross development, test, and production environments. Using ALM pipelines such as Power Platform\ndeployment pipelines or Azure DevOps pipelines ensures consistent and automated deployment processes\nwhile maintaining enterprise governance standards.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Deploy the APIs as Azure Functions.\nThis is incorrect because deploying APIs as Azure Functions does not address lifecycle management and\ngovernance of the Power Platform custom connectors themselves.\nC. Maintain connector definitions in environment variables.\nThis is incorrect because environment variables store configuration values and endpoints, not full connector\nlifecycle management and deployment definitions.\nD. Export and import the connectors between the environments as unmanaged solutions\nThis is incorrect because unmanaged solutions are not recommended for controlled enterprise ALM\nprocesses and reduce governance and traceability capabilities.","sourcePages":[35,36],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":33,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform environment.\nYou need to build two agents named Agent1 and Agent2. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Agent1 must be extendable by using the Semantic Kernel and must connect to multiple business\napps and APIs.\n• Agent2 must connect directly to data stored in Microsoft Dataverse and must be embeddable in a\nMicrosoft Power Apps canvas app.\nWhat should you use to build each agent?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform environment.\nYou need to build two agents named Agent1 and Agent2. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Agent1 must be extendable by using the Semantic Kernel and must connect to multiple business\napps and APIs.\n• Agent2 must connect directly to data stored in Microsoft Dataverse and must be embeddable in a\nMicrosoft Power Apps canvas app.\nWhat should you use to build each agent?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Foundry for Agent1\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio for Agent2","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Foundry for Agent1\nThis option is correct because Agent1 must be extendable by using Semantic Kernel and connect to multiple\nbusiness applications and APIs. Microsoft Foundry is designed for pro-code and enterprise AI development\nscenarios that require advanced orchestration, extensibility, and integration capabilities. It natively supports\nSemantic Kernel integration, enabling developers to build sophisticated AI agents with custom orchestration,\nmemory, plugins, and API connectivity across enterprise systems.\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio for Agent2\nThis option is correct because Agent2 must connect directly to Microsoft Dataverse and be embeddable\ninside a Power Apps canvas app. Copilot Studio is built natively on the Power Platform and provides\nseamless Dataverse integration, Power Platform connector support, and native embedding capabilities for\nPower Apps. It is specifically optimized for low-code conversational AI solutions within the Microsoft\nbusiness applications ecosystem.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure Logic Apps\nThis is incorrect because Logic Apps is primarily an integration and workflow automation platform rather than\nan AI agent development platform.\nCopilot in Power Apps\nThis is incorrect because Copilot in Power Apps refers to embedded AI assistance features within Power\nApps rather than a standalone platform for building deployable AI agents.\nMicrosoft Foundry for Agent2\nThis is incorrect because Foundry introduces unnecessary complexity for a low-code Dataverse-connected\nPower Apps embedding scenario.","sourcePages":[36,37],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":34,"stem":"A company has Microsoft Foundry agents that generate responses by using Azure OpenAI resources.\nThe agents are deployed to both the United States and Europe.\nA company mandate states that the agents and their grounding data must adhere to data residency and\nmovement regulations.\nYou need to recommend a governance solution for the agents.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company has Microsoft Foundry agents that generate responses by using Azure OpenAI resources.\nThe agents are deployed to both the United States and Europe.\nA company mandate states that the agents and their grounding data must adhere to data residency and\nmovement regulations.\nYou need to recommend a governance solution for the agents.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Microsoft Defender for Cloud\nB. Azure Policy\nC. Azure Monitor\nD. Microsoft Purview","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure Policy"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure Monitor"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Purview"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Microsoft Purview","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Microsoft Purview\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Purview provides enterprise data governance, compliance, and data\nresidency management capabilities. It helps organizations classify, govern, monitor, and control sensitive data\nacross AI systems and enterprise environments. In this scenario, the requirement specifically focuses on\nensuring that agents and grounding data comply with data residency and data movement regulations across\nthe United States and Europe. Microsoft Purview supports these governance requirements through data\nclassification, compliance policies, data lineage, auditing, and regulatory compliance management.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Microsoft Defender for Cloud\nThis is incorrect because Defender for Cloud focuses primarily on security posture management and threat\nprotection rather than data residency governance.\nB. Azure Policy\nThis is incorrect because Azure Policy can enforce deployment restrictions such as allowed regions, but it\ndoes not provide comprehensive data governance and data movement compliance management for\ngrounding data.\nC. Azure Monitor\nThis is incorrect because Azure Monitor provides telemetry and operational monitoring rather than\ngovernance or compliance management.","sourcePages":[38],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":35,"stem":"A company has an AI solution built by using Microsoft Copilot Studio and Power Platform. The solution\nis used by the company's sales, marketing, and customer service teams.\nYou are performing a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis to evaluate the impact of the solution. You\nneed to identify which measurable business drivers to include in the analysis.\nWhich two business drivers should you identify?","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI solution built by using Microsoft Copilot Studio and Power Platform. The solution\nis used by the company's sales, marketing, and customer service teams.\nYou are performing a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis to evaluate the impact of the solution. You\nneed to identify which measurable business drivers to include in the analysis.\nWhich two business drivers should you identify?\nA. the reduced average case resolution time\nB. market capitalization\nC. economic market predictability\nD. increased employee productivity\nE. brand awareness","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"the reduced average case resolution time"},{"label":"B","text":"market capitalization"},{"label":"C","text":"economic market predictability"},{"label":"D","text":"increased employee productivity"},{"label":"E","text":"brand awareness"}],"correct":["A","D"],"answer":"A. the reduced average case resolution time\nD. increased employee productivity","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. the reduced average case resolution time\nThis option is correct because reduced case resolution time is a measurable operational improvement\ndirectly tied to AI-driven automation and assistance capabilities. Faster issue resolution improves customer\nservice efficiency, reduces operational costs, and increases overall business effectiveness. This is a key\nmeasurable business driver commonly used in return on AI investment analyses.\nD. increased employee productivity\nThis option is correct because AI solutions built with Copilot Studio and Power Platform are designed to\nautomate repetitive tasks, accelerate workflows, and improve employee efficiency across departments such\nas sales, marketing, and customer service. Increased employee productivity is one of the primary\nmeasurable business outcomes used to evaluate the value and impact of AI investments.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. market capitalization\nThis is incorrect because market capitalization is influenced by many external financial and economic factors\nand is not a direct measurable driver for evaluating AI solution impact.\nC. economic market predictability\nThis is incorrect because it is a broad macroeconomic concept rather than a measurable business outcome\ndirectly attributable to the AI solution.\nE. brand awareness\nThis is incorrect because brand awareness is difficult to directly quantify as a return on AI investment metric\nfor operational AI business solutions.","sourcePages":[38,39],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":36,"stem":"A company has a cloud-based AI solution that uses Azure OpenAI models.\nYou need to design a monitoring solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Monitors performance metrics and operational health for the models\n• Monitors AI apps and agents for compliance\n• Uses Azure-native capabilities\n• Minimizes development effort\nWhat should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct\nrequirements. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"A company has a cloud-based AI solution that uses Azure OpenAI models.\nYou need to design a monitoring solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Monitors performance metrics and operational health for the models\n• Monitors AI apps and agents for compliance\n• Uses Azure-native capabilities\n• Minimizes development effort\nWhat should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct\nrequirements. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Purview\nAzure Monitor","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Purview\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Purview provides governance, compliance monitoring, auditing, data\nclassification, and policy management capabilities for AI applications and agents. It helps organizations\nmonitor AI systems for compliance requirements, track data usage, detect policy violations, and support\nresponsible AI governance across enterprise AI environments.\nAzure Monitor\nThis option is correct because Azure Monitor is the Azure-native service used for monitoring operational\nhealth, telemetry, diagnostics, performance metrics, and availability of cloud-based applications and Azure\nOpenAI workloads. It provides dashboards, alerts, logs, and analytics to monitor AI model performance and\noperational reliability while minimizing development effort through built-in Azure integrations.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure API Management\nThis is incorrect because API Management focuses on API publishing, security, and traffic management\nrather than operational monitoring or AI governance.\nAzure Policy\nThis is incorrect because Azure Policy enforces governance rules and deployment compliance but does not\nprovide operational monitoring or AI compliance analytics.\nMicrosoft Defender\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Defender primarily focuses on security threat detection and protection\nrather than broader AI compliance governance.\nAzure Stream Analytics\nThis is incorrect because Stream Analytics is designed for real-time event stream processing rather than AI\nmonitoring and governance.","sourcePages":[39,40,41],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":37,"stem":"A company has a canvas app named App1 in a Microsoft Power Platform environment named Env1.\nEnv1 uses a customer-managed key for data encryption. App1 connects to multiple data sources to retrieve\nand update customer and order information.\nYou need to recommend a solution to add Microsoft Copilot components to App1. The solution must NOT\nmodify the current security or encryption configurations of Env1.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company has a canvas app named App1 in a Microsoft Power Platform environment named Env1.\nEnv1 uses a customer-managed key for data encryption. App1 connects to multiple data sources to retrieve\nand update customer and order information.\nYou need to recommend a solution to add Microsoft Copilot components to App1. The solution must NOT\nmodify the current security or encryption configurations of Env1.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Modify the data sources of App1 to make them compatible with Copilot.\nB. Duplicate App1 and republish the app in Env1.\nC. Enable Copilot features for Env1.\nD. Move App1 to a new environment that uses Microsoft-managed keys.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Modify the data sources of App1 to make them compatible with Copilot."},{"label":"B","text":"Duplicate App1 and republish the app in Env1."},{"label":"C","text":"Enable Copilot features for Env1."},{"label":"D","text":"Move App1 to a new environment that uses Microsoft-managed keys."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Enable Copilot features for Env1.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Enable Copilot features for Env1.\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Copilot features can be enabled within a Power Platform\nenvironment that already uses customer-managed keys for encryption. Enabling Copilot capabilities at the\nenvironment level allows App1 to use Copilot components without requiring changes to the existing security\narchitecture, encryption configuration, or data protection settings. This satisfies the requirement to preserve\nthe current customer-managed key configuration in Env1.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Modify the data sources of App1 to make them compatible with Copilot.\nThis is incorrect because Copilot integration does not require modifying existing compatible data sources\nsolely due to customer-managed encryption keys.\nB. Duplicate App1 and republish the app in Env1.\nThis is incorrect because duplicating the app does not address enabling Copilot functionality or provide any\nsecurity or encryption benefit.\nD. Move App1 to a new environment that uses Microsoft-managed keys.\nThis is incorrect because the requirement explicitly states that the current security and encryption\nconfiguration must not be modified.","sourcePages":[41],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":38,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 to manage service operations. Dispatchers coordinate service\nrequests, and technicians perform scheduled on-site work.\nYou need to design a solution that will use Microsoft Copilot to improve the efficiency of the service\noperations. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Provide AI-driven assistance to help staff organize and resolve work orders.\n• Deliver contextual AI support to frontline workers as they prepare for and complete customer\nappointments.\nWhich two components should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 to manage service operations. Dispatchers coordinate service\nrequests, and technicians perform scheduled on-site work.\nYou need to design a solution that will use Microsoft Copilot to improve the efficiency of the service\noperations. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Provide AI-driven assistance to help staff organize and resolve work orders.\n• Deliver contextual AI support to frontline workers as they prepare for and complete customer\nappointments.\nWhich two components should you include in the design?\nA. Copilot Service workspace\nB. Copilot in Outlook\nC. Dynamics 365 Customer Service\nD. Copilot in Customer Service\nE. Copilot in Field Service\nF. the Dynamics 365 Field Service mobile app","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Copilot Service workspace"},{"label":"B","text":"Copilot in Outlook"},{"label":"C","text":"Dynamics 365 Customer Service"},{"label":"D","text":"Copilot in Customer Service"},{"label":"E","text":"Copilot in Field Service"},{"label":"F","text":"the Dynamics 365 Field Service mobile app"}],"correct":["E","F"],"answer":"E. Copilot in Field Service\nF. the Dynamics 365 Field Service mobile app","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nE. Copilot in Field Service\nThis option is correct because Copilot in Field Service provides AI-driven assistance for dispatchers and\nservice managers to organize, manage, and resolve work orders more efficiently. It delivers AI-generated\nsummaries, scheduling assistance, service recommendations, and operational insights that help improve\nservice coordination and work order management within Dynamics 365 Field Service.\nF. the Dynamics 365 Field Service mobile app\nThis option is correct because the Dynamics 365 Field Service mobile app provides frontline technicians with\ncontextual support while preparing for and completing customer appointments. When integrated with Copilot\ncapabilities, the mobile app enables technicians to access work order summaries, appointment details, task\nguidance, voice-driven updates, and operational insights directly from their mobile devices during onsite\nservice activities.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Copilot Service workspace\nThis is incorrect because it is primarily a workspace interface and not the core operational Field Service AI\nsolution required for dispatchers and technicians.\nB. Copilot in Outlook\nThis is incorrect because Outlook Copilot focuses on email productivity rather than field service operations\nand onsite technician workflows.\nC. Dynamics 365 Customer Service\nThis is incorrect because Customer Service is focused on contact center and support case management\nscenarios rather than field dispatch and onsite service operations.\nD. Copilot in Customer Service\nThis is incorrect because the scenario specifically focuses on field service operations, dispatchers, work\norders, and technicians rather than customer support agents and case management.","sourcePages":[42,43],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":39,"stem":"A company uses Azure OpenAI models that use grounding data from Microsoft Fabric for agents.\nThe models are fine-tuned by using proprietary datasets.\nYou need to design a governance solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Restricts access to the grounding data to only assigned roles\n• Restricts model fine-tuning to only the AI engineering team\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Azure OpenAI models that use grounding data from Microsoft Fabric for agents.\nThe models are fine-tuned by using proprietary datasets.\nYou need to design a governance solution that meets the following requirements:\n• Restricts access to the grounding data to only assigned roles\n• Restricts model fine-tuning to only the AI engineering team\nWhat should you include in the design?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Purview access policies\nRole-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Purview access policies\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Purview provides enterprise data governance and access control\ncapabilities for grounding data stored in Microsoft Fabric and other enterprise data platforms. Purview access\npolicies help ensure that only assigned users and roles can access sensitive grounding datasets used by\nAzure OpenAI agents. This supports secure data governance and controlled access to proprietary enterprise\ninformation.\nRole-based access control (RBAC) in Microsoft Foundry\nThis option is correct because RBAC in Microsoft Foundry allows organizations to restrict AI model fine-\ntuning activities to specific users or groups such as the AI engineering team. RBAC enables granular\npermission management for AI resources, training workflows, and fine-tuning operations, ensuring that only\nauthorized personnel can modify or retrain models.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure AI Content Safety\nThis is incorrect because Content Safety focuses on harmful content moderation rather than access control\nfor grounding data.\nAzure Monitor alerts\nThis is incorrect because Azure Monitor provides telemetry and alerting rather than data access governance.\nAzure Policy compliance rules\nThis is incorrect because Azure Policy enforces configuration governance but does not provide detailed role-\nbased data access management for grounding datasets.\nAzure Resource Manager (ARM) resource locks\nThis is incorrect because ARM locks prevent accidental resource deletion or modification but do not control\nfine-tuning permissions.\nMicrosoft Entra Conditional Access\nThis is incorrect because Conditional Access controls authentication conditions and sign-in policies rather\nthan AI fine-tuning authorization management.","sourcePages":[43,44,45],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":40,"stem":"A company has a customer order system that creates sales orders manually.\nYou need to design an AI solution to automate the following tasks as part of the system:\n• Save the order details to a database.\n• Update the order status in the database.\n• Extract the order details from an order file.\n• Prepare and send a confirmation email to customers.\nThe solution must minimize development effort and support intelligent automation and solution integration.\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A company has a customer order system that creates sales orders manually.\nYou need to design an AI solution to automate the following tasks as part of the system:\n• Save the order details to a database.\n• Update the order status in the database.\n• Extract the order details from an order file.\n• Prepare and send a confirmation email to customers.\nThe solution must minimize development effort and support intelligent automation and solution integration.\nWhat should you include in the design?\nA. a workflow in Azure Logic Apps\nB. a multi-agent solution that uses the Semantic Kernel SDK\nC. a multi-agent solution that uses Microsoft Foundry Agent Service\nD. a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses Microsoft Power Automate workflows","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"a workflow in Azure Logic Apps"},{"label":"B","text":"a multi-agent solution that uses the Semantic Kernel SDK"},{"label":"C","text":"a multi-agent solution that uses Microsoft Foundry Agent Service"},{"label":"D","text":"a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses Microsoft Power Automate workflows"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses Microsoft Power Automate workflows","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses Microsoft Power Automate workflows\nThis option is correct because the requirement focuses on minimizing development effort while supporting\nintelligent automation and integration across business processes. Microsoft Copilot Studio combined with\nPower Automate provides a low-code solution for automating workflows such as extracting order details from\nfiles, updating databases, managing order statuses, and sending confirmation emails. Power Automate offers\nbuilt-in connectors and workflow orchestration capabilities, while Copilot Studio adds intelligent AI-driven\ninteractions and automation support. This combination is specifically designed for business process\nautomation scenarios with minimal custom development effort.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. a workflow in Azure Logic Apps\nThis is incorrect because although Logic Apps supports workflow automation and integrations, it does not\nprovide the same low-code AI agent capabilities and business-focused intelligent automation experience as\nCopilot Studio combined with Power Automate.\nB. a multi-agent solution that uses the Semantic Kernel SDK\nThis is incorrect because Semantic Kernel is a pro-code development framework and would require\nsignificantly more custom development effort.\nC. a multi-agent solution that uses Microsoft Foundry Agent Service\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Foundry Agent Service is intended for advanced enterprise AI agent\norchestration scenarios and introduces unnecessary complexity for this business automation requirement.","sourcePages":[45],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":41,"stem":"A manufacturing company wants to deploy an agent that will automate supplier invoice processing.\nYou are designing a solution to evaluate the financial implications of the deployment. The company is\nespecially concerned about budget overruns.\nYou need to ensure that the solution considers the total cost of ownership (TCO), the expected savings from\nusing automation, and whether to extend the existing AI capabilities.\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A manufacturing company wants to deploy an agent that will automate supplier invoice processing.\nYou are designing a solution to evaluate the financial implications of the deployment. The company is\nespecially concerned about budget overruns.\nYou need to ensure that the solution considers the total cost of ownership (TCO), the expected savings from\nusing automation, and whether to extend the existing AI capabilities.\nWhat should you include in the design?\nA. a break-even analysis only\nB. adopting prebuilt agents to reduce the deployment time\nC. training a custom model\nD. a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"a break-even analysis only"},{"label":"B","text":"adopting prebuilt agents to reduce the deployment time"},{"label":"C","text":"training a custom model"},{"label":"D","text":"a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nD. a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis\nThis option is correct because the scenario requires evaluating the complete financial impact of deploying an\nAI agent.\nThe requirements explicitly mention:\ntotal cost of ownership (TCO),\nexpected savings from automation,\nand whether extending existing AI capabilities is financially justified.\nA Return on AI Investment (ROAI) analysis is specifically designed to assess AI initiatives by considering\ncosts, benefits, risks, savings, productivity gains, and long-term business value.\nROAI provides a comprehensive framework for determining whether the investment will generate sufficient\nbusiness value while helping identify and mitigate the risk of budget overruns.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. a break-even analysis only\nThis option is incorrect because a break-even analysis focuses only on determining when costs equal\nbenefits. It does not provide a complete assessment of AI value, TCO, strategic benefits, or future AI\ncapability expansion.\nB. adopting prebuilt agents to reduce the deployment time\nThis option is incorrect because using prebuilt agents may reduce implementation effort, but it does not\nevaluate the overall financial implications of the deployment.\nC. training a custom model\nThis option is incorrect because training a custom model is an implementation decision rather than a financial\nevaluation methodology. In fact, it may increase costs and does not address the requirement to assess TCO\nand expected savings.","sourcePages":[46],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":42,"stem":"A company has an AI solution named Solution1 that is deployed to the production environment.\nSolution1 uses an Azure OpenAI model to generate marketing emails for existing customers.\nDuring an internal review, you identify that Solution1 creates different emails depending on the customers'\ntraits.\nYou need to recommend a strategy to mitigate the bias. The strategy must adhere to Microsoft responsible AI\nprinciples.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI solution named Solution1 that is deployed to the production environment.\nSolution1 uses an Azure OpenAI model to generate marketing emails for existing customers.\nDuring an internal review, you identify that Solution1 creates different emails depending on the customers'\ntraits.\nYou need to recommend a strategy to mitigate the bias. The strategy must adhere to Microsoft responsible AI\nprinciples.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Modify Solution1 to randomly generate emails for different traits.\nB. Modify the system instructions of Solution1.\nC. Retrain the model by using a larger dataset.\nD. Modify the contents of the training dataset.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Modify Solution1 to randomly generate emails for different traits."},{"label":"B","text":"Modify the system instructions of Solution1."},{"label":"C","text":"Retrain the model by using a larger dataset."},{"label":"D","text":"Modify the contents of the training dataset."}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Modify the system instructions of Solution1.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Modify the system instructions of Solution1.\nThis option is correct because Solution1 uses an Azure OpenAI foundation model provided as a managed\nservice. In Azure OpenAI solutions, one of the primary and recommended approaches for mitigating biased\noutputs is modifying the system instructions or metaprompts that guide model behavior. By updating the\nsystem instructions, the company can enforce fairness constraints, standardize tone and messaging, reduce\ndiscriminatory behavior, and align the generated content with Microsoft Responsible AI principles such as\nfairness, inclusiveness, and reliability. This provides an immediate and governance-aligned mitigation strategy\nwithout retraining the underlying model.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Modify Solution1 to randomly generate emails for different traits.\nThis is incorrect because randomization does not address the root cause of the bias and can introduce\ninconsistent and unreliable customer experiences.\nC. Retrain the model by using a larger dataset.\nThis is incorrect because Azure OpenAI foundation models are managed pretrained models, and simply\nincreasing dataset size does not guarantee removal of bias.\nD. Modify the contents of the training dataset.\nThis is incorrect because customers typically do not modify the underlying training datasets of Azure OpenAI\nfoundation models. Bias mitigation in Azure OpenAI solutions is usually implemented through prompt\nengineering, system instructions, and governance controls at the application layer.","sourcePages":[47],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":43,"stem":"You need to recommend a Microsoft Power Platform solution for customer support.\nThe solution must include AI capabilities in Microsoft Power Automate and must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• Use a centralized workspace for AI models.\n• Generate short overviews from large amounts of unstructured text, such as case notes or transcripts,\nwithout requiring additional training or coding.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"You need to recommend a Microsoft Power Platform solution for customer support.\nThe solution must include AI capabilities in Microsoft Power Automate and must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• Use a centralized workspace for AI models.\n• Generate short overviews from large amounts of unstructured text, such as case notes or transcripts,\nwithout requiring additional training or coding.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"A Microsoft Foundry hub\nAn AI Builder prebuilt prompt","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA Microsoft Foundry hub\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Foundry hubs provide a centralized workspace for managing AI\nresources, models, projects, and governance capabilities across AI solutions. It enables centralized AI model\nmanagement and supports enterprise AI development and orchestration scenarios within Microsoft\necosystems.\nAn AI Builder prebuilt prompt\nThis option is correct because AI Builder prebuilt prompts can generate summaries and short overviews from\nlarge amounts of unstructured text such as transcripts, customer conversations, and case notes without\nrequiring additional model training or custom coding. This aligns directly with the requirement for low-code\nAI summarization capabilities within Microsoft Power Automate.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure OpenAI Foundry\nThis is incorrect because the centralized AI workspace capability is specifically provided through Microsoft\nFoundry hubs.\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio\nThis is incorrect because Copilot Studio focuses on agent and conversational AI creation rather than\ncentralized AI model workspace management.\nMicrosoft Dataverse\nThis is incorrect because Dataverse is primarily a business data platform rather than a centralized AI model\nworkspace.\nAn AI Builder prebuilt model\nThis is incorrect because prebuilt models are task-specific AI models, while summarization of arbitrary\nunstructured text is better aligned with prebuilt prompts.\nAzure OpenAI\nThis is incorrect because although Azure OpenAI supports summarization, the question specifically\nemphasizes minimizing coding and training effort within Power Platform.\nGitHub Copilot\nThis is incorrect because GitHub Copilot focuses on developer coding assistance rather than business text\nsummarization workflows.\nMicrosoft Copilot in Power Automate\nThis is incorrect because Copilot assists flow creation rather than directly serving as the summarization\ncapability requested in the requirement.","sourcePages":[48,49,50],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":44,"stem":"You need to design a Microsoft 365 Copilot solution to optimize employee productivity.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the employees can query content stored in a subset of Microsoft SharePoint Online sites\nand in Teams by using natural language-based prompt actions.\n• Ensure that employees receive contextually relevant responses in Microsoft 365 Copilot.\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"You need to design a Microsoft 365 Copilot solution to optimize employee productivity.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the employees can query content stored in a subset of Microsoft SharePoint Online sites\nand in Teams by using natural language-based prompt actions.\n• Ensure that employees receive contextually relevant responses in Microsoft 365 Copilot.\nWhat should you include in the design?\nA. Build a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to read the SharePoint content and post the responses to\nTeams.\nB. Modify SharePoint settings.\nC. Create a custom REST API that crawls the SharePoint content.\nD. Configure Microsoft Graph access.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Build a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to read the SharePoint content and post the responses to Teams."},{"label":"B","text":"Modify SharePoint settings."},{"label":"C","text":"Create a custom REST API that crawls the SharePoint content."},{"label":"D","text":"Configure Microsoft Graph access."}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Configure Microsoft Graph access.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Configure Microsoft Graph access.\nThis option is correct because Microsoft 365 Copilot relies on Microsoft Graph to access organizational data\nstored across Microsoft 365 services such as SharePoint Online and Microsoft Teams. Microsoft Graph\nprovides the contextual grounding and semantic understanding required for natural language queries and\ncontextually relevant responses. By configuring Microsoft Graph access appropriately, employees can query\nauthorized SharePoint and Teams content through Microsoft 365 Copilot while maintaining security trimming\nand contextual relevance.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Build a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to read the SharePoint content and post the responses\nto Teams.\nThis is incorrect because Power Automate desktop flows are not required for Microsoft 365 Copilot\ngrounding or contextual response generation.\nB. Modify SharePoint settings.\nThis is incorrect because SharePoint settings alone do not provide the Microsoft 365 Copilot semantic access\nand contextual grounding capabilities required for natural language queries.\nC. Create a custom REST API that crawls the SharePoint content.\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft 365 Copilot already uses Microsoft Graph as the native enterprise data\naccess layer, making custom crawling solutions unnecessary.","sourcePages":[50,51],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":45,"stem":"You need to design a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for customer support.\nThe agent must securely retrieve product warranty data from a REST API. The solution must minimize\ndevelopment effort.\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"You need to design a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for customer support.\nThe agent must securely retrieve product warranty data from a REST API. The solution must minimize\ndevelopment effort.\nWhat should you include in the design?\nA. Export the agent as a managed solution and customize the agent in Power Apps.\nB. Create a custom connector in Copilot Studio and use the connector to call the API.\nC. Use a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to screen scrape the warranty data.\nD. Add the warranty data to the Fallback topic.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Export the agent as a managed solution and customize the agent in Power Apps."},{"label":"B","text":"Create a custom connector in Copilot Studio and use the connector to call the API."},{"label":"C","text":"Use a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to screen scrape the warranty data."},{"label":"D","text":"Add the warranty data to the Fallback topic."}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Create a custom connector in Copilot Studio and use the connector to call the API.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Create a custom connector in Copilot Studio and use the connector to call the API.\nThis option is correct because custom connectors in Microsoft Copilot Studio are specifically designed to\nsecurely integrate external REST APIs into agents with minimal development effort. A custom connector\nallows the agent to authenticate securely, call the warranty REST API, retrieve product warranty information,\nand use the data within conversational workflows. This provides a low-code integration approach aligned with\nPower Platform best practices.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Export the agent as a managed solution and customize the agent in Power Apps.\nThis is incorrect because exporting and customizing the agent does not provide REST API integration\nfunctionality.\nC. Use a Microsoft Power Automate desktop flow to screen scrape the warranty data.\nThis is incorrect because screen scraping is inefficient, fragile, and unnecessary when a REST API already\nexists.\nD. Add the warranty data to the Fallback topic.\nThis is incorrect because the Fallback topic is intended for handling unrecognized conversations and cannot\ndynamically retrieve secure API data.","sourcePages":[51],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":46,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that has been in production for three months. The\nagent has received positive feedback from users.\nYou need to identify the number of questions unanswered by the agent and the number of abandoned\nsessions between the users and the agent.\nWhich Copilot Studio insights should you use?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate insights to the correct requirements. Each insight may be used once, more\nthan once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that has been in production for three months. The\nagent has received positive feedback from users.\nYou need to identify the number of questions unanswered by the agent and the number of abandoned\nsessions between the users and the agent.\nWhich Copilot Studio insights should you use?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate insights to the correct requirements. Each insight may be used once, more\nthan once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Generated answer rate and quality","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nGenerated answer rate and quality\nThis insight is correct for identifying unanswered questions because it measures how effectively the Copilot\nStudio agent generates responses and evaluates the quality of those generated answers. It helps identify\nsituations where the agent could not provide useful or accurate answers, which directly relates to\nunanswered user questions.\nConversation outcomes\nThis insight is correct for identifying abandoned sessions because it tracks how conversations end, including\ncompleted conversations, escalations, failures, and abandoned sessions. It provides visibility into user\ninteractions that ended before resolution or completion.\nIncorrect Answers:\nReactions\nThis is incorrect because reactions primarily measure user sentiment and feedback such as likes or dislikes\nrather than unanswered questions or abandoned sessions.\nSurvey results\nThis is incorrect because surveys provide customer satisfaction feedback but do not directly measure\nunanswered questions or abandoned session counts.","sourcePages":[51,52,53],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":47,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry generative AI model.\nYou need to evaluate the model's output to measure the overall quality and coherence of generated\nresponses. The evaluation must use GPT-4o as a judge and return a numeric score for each output.\nWhich type of metric should you use?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry generative AI model.\nYou need to evaluate the model's output to measure the overall quality and coherence of generated\nresponses. The evaluation must use GPT-4o as a judge and return a numeric score for each output.\nWhich type of metric should you use?\nA. AI quality (NLP)\nB. risk and safety\nC. Groundedness\nD. AI quality (AI assisted)","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"AI quality (NLP)"},{"label":"B","text":"risk and safety"},{"label":"C","text":"Groundedness"},{"label":"D","text":"AI quality (AI assisted)"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. AI quality (AI assisted)","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. AI quality (AI assisted)\nThis option is correct because AI quality (AI assisted) metrics use advanced AI models such as GPT-4o as\nevaluators or judges to assess generated responses. These metrics evaluate factors such as coherence,\nrelevance, fluency, completeness, and overall response quality, and they return numeric scores for each\ngenerated output. This aligns exactly with the requirement to use GPT-4o as a judge for evaluating generative\nAI responses.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. AI quality (NLP)\nThis is incorrect because AI quality (NLP) metrics typically use traditional natural language processing\nevaluation methods rather than GPT-4o-assisted evaluation.\nB. risk and safety\nThis is incorrect because risk and safety metrics focus on harmful content, bias, toxicity, jailbreak attempts,\nand safety violations rather than overall response quality and coherence.\nC. Groundedness\nThis is incorrect because groundedness measures whether responses are supported by the provided\ngrounding data, not overall response quality or coherence.\nNote: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each\nquestion presents a unique solution to the problem. You must determine whether the solution meets the\nstated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also possible that none of the\nsolutions in the set solve the problem.\nIn real exam, after you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these\nquestions do not appear on the Review Screen in exam.","sourcePages":[53,54],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":48,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled.\nYou need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are\npresented to users.\nSolution: You add fields to the opportunity summary. Does this meet the goal?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled.\nYou need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are\npresented to users.\nSolution: You add fields to the opportunity summary. Does this meet the goal?\nA. Yes\nB. No","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Yes","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Yes\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales Copilot opportunity summaries can be\ncustomized by modifying the fields included in the summary experience. By adding or configuring fields,\norganizations can tailor the generated summaries to display business-specific information that is more\nrelevant to users. This directly affects how the summary content is generated and presented, helping align\nCopilot outputs with organizational requirements and sales processes.","sourcePages":[54],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":49,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled.\nYou need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are\npresented to users.\nSolution: You build Microsoft Power Automate flows to trigger customized Copilot summaries. Does this\nmeet the goal?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled.\nYou need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are\npresented to users.\nSolution: You build Microsoft Power Automate flows to trigger customized Copilot summaries. Does this\nmeet the goal?\nA. Yes\nB. No","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. No","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. No\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Power Automate flows are used for workflow automation and\nprocess orchestration, but they do not directly customize how Microsoft Copilot generates or presents\nopportunity summaries in Dynamics 365 Sales. Opportunity summary customization is handled through\nsupported Copilot configuration and customization capabilities within Dynamics 365 Sales, such as\nconfiguring summary fields and presentation settings. Building Power Automate flows does not modify the\nnative Copilot summary generation logic.","sourcePages":[54,55],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":50,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled.\nYou need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are\npresented to users.\nSolution: You configure AI Builder lead scoring models to influence opportunity summaries. Does this meet\nthe goal?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales environment that has Microsoft Copilot enabled.\nYou need to customize Copilot by tailoring how opportunity summaries are generated or how they are\npresented to users.\nSolution: You configure AI Builder lead scoring models to influence opportunity summaries. Does this meet\nthe goal?\nA. Yes\nB. No","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"No"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. No","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. No\nThis option is correct because AI Builder lead scoring models are used to predict and score leads based on\nlikelihood of conversion or other business criteria. They are intended for predictive analytics and sales\nprioritization scenarios, not for customizing how Microsoft Copilot generates or presents opportunity\nsummaries in Dynamics 365 Sales. Configuring lead scoring models does not influence the structure,\nformatting, or content generation logic of Copilot opportunity summaries.","sourcePages":[55],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":51,"stem":"A company has an AI solution that uses Azure OpenAI models.\nYou need to recommend a governance solution that monitors and audits changes to model configurations\nand data usage. The solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI solution that uses Azure OpenAI models.\nYou need to recommend a governance solution that monitors and audits changes to model configurations\nand data usage. The solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Azure Monitor\nB. Azure Stream Analytics\nC. Azure API Management\nD. Azure Policy\nE. Microsoft Purview","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Azure Monitor"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure Stream Analytics"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure API Management"},{"label":"D","text":"Azure Policy"},{"label":"E","text":"Microsoft Purview"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Azure Monitor","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Azure Monitor\nThis option is correct because Azure Monitor provides native monitoring, logging, telemetry, and auditing\ncapabilities for Azure OpenAI resources. By enabling diagnostic settings, Azure Monitor can automatically\ncapture model deployment changes, configuration updates, request logs, token usage metrics, latency, and\noperational health information. This provides built-in monitoring and auditing with minimal administrative\neffort, making it the most appropriate solution for tracking model configurations and data usage in Azure\nOpenAI environments.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Azure Stream Analytics\nThis is incorrect because Azure Stream Analytics is designed for real-time event processing and streaming\nanalytics rather than AI model monitoring and auditing.\nC. Azure API Management\nThis is incorrect because API Management focuses on API governance, security, and traffic management\nrather than native Azure OpenAI model configuration auditing and telemetry monitoring.\nD. Azure Policy\nThis is incorrect because Azure Policy enforces compliance rules and deployment governance but does not\nprovide detailed operational monitoring or auditing of model usage and configuration changes.\nE. Microsoft Purview\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Purview primarily focuses on enterprise data governance, compliance,\nclassification, and responsible AI governance rather than operational monitoring of Azure OpenAI resources\nand telemetry.","sourcePages":[55,56],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":52,"stem":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center agent.\nYou need to ensure that the agent can transfer the conversation to a live customer service representative.\nWhich two components should you include in the solution?","rawQuestion":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Dynamics 365 Contact Center agent.\nYou need to ensure that the agent can transfer the conversation to a live customer service representative.\nWhich two components should you include in the solution?\nA. Microsoft Foundry\nB. Microsoft Copilot Studio\nC. Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit\nD. an Azure AI Bot Service skill\nE. Customer engagement hub","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Foundry"},{"label":"B","text":"Microsoft Copilot Studio"},{"label":"C","text":"Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit"},{"label":"D","text":"an Azure AI Bot Service skill"},{"label":"E","text":"Customer engagement hub"}],"correct":["B","E"],"answer":"B. Microsoft Copilot Studio\nE. Customer engagement hub","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Copilot Studio is used to build and configure conversational agents\nfor Dynamics 365 Contact Center. It supports escalation and handoff scenarios where AI agents can transfer\nconversations to live customer service representatives when human intervention is required.\nE. Customer engagement hub\nThis option is correct because the Customer engagement hub provides the Dynamics 365 customer service\nenvironment where live customer service representatives manage and continue customer conversations after\nescalation from the AI agent. It enables seamless human-agent collaboration during customer support\ninteractions.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Microsoft Foundry\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Foundry is intended for advanced AI development and orchestration\nrather than Dynamics 365 Contact Center escalation workflows.\nC. Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit\nThis is incorrect because the Agents Toolkit is designed for Microsoft 365 extensibility and agent\ndevelopment scenarios rather than Contact Center live-agent escalation.\nD. an Azure AI Bot Service skill\nThis is incorrect because Bot Service skills extend bot capabilities but are not required for transferring\nconversations to live representatives in Dynamics 365 Contact Center.","sourcePages":[56,57],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":53,"stem":"You use Microsoft Copilot Studio analytics to analyze the performance of a deployed Copilot Studio\nagent.\nYou need to identify which performance metrics to use to measure the following:\n• The percentage of engaged sessions that are escalated to a live customer service representative\n• The number of agent queries that cause a knowledge source error\nWhat should you identify for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"You use Microsoft Copilot Studio analytics to analyze the performance of a deployed Copilot Studio\nagent.\nYou need to identify which performance metrics to use to measure the following:\n• The percentage of engaged sessions that are escalated to a live customer service representative\n• The number of agent queries that cause a knowledge source error\nWhat should you identify for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Escalation rate","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nEscalation rate\nThis metric is correct because escalation rate measures the percentage of engaged sessions that are\ntransferred or escalated from the Copilot Studio agent to a live customer service representative. It directly\ntracks how often the agent cannot fully resolve customer interactions independently.\nAnswer quality\nThis metric is correct because answer quality helps identify issues with generated responses, including\nsituations where the agent fails to retrieve or use knowledge source information correctly. Knowledge source\nerrors negatively impact generated answer quality, making this the most appropriate metric for identifying\nproblematic queries related to grounding or retrieval failures.\nIncorrect Answers:\nCustomer Satisfaction (CSAT) score\nThis is incorrect because CSAT measures user satisfaction rather than escalation percentages or knowledge\nsource failures.\nCopilot in Power Apps\nThis is incorrect because it is a product capability rather than an analytics metric.\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio\nThis is incorrect because it is the platform itself rather than a specific performance metric.\nEngagement rate\nThis is incorrect because engagement rate measures how actively users interact with the agent, not\nescalation behavior or knowledge source errors.","sourcePages":[57,58],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":54,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales to manage leads that are stored in a Microsoft\nDataverse table named Lead and use non-standard terminology and custom columns.\nYou need to configure business terms in the Lead table so that Microsoft Copilot controls can summarize the\nleads efficiently. The solution must minimize administrative effort.\nHow should you configure the business terms?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales to manage leads that are stored in a Microsoft\nDataverse table named Lead and use non-standard terminology and custom columns.\nYou need to configure business terms in the Lead table so that Microsoft Copilot controls can summarize the\nleads efficiently. The solution must minimize administrative effort.\nHow should you configure the business terms?\nA. Combine all the fields into one custom field.\nB. Map the field display names as business terms.\nC. Add the schema names as business terms.\nD. Create new business terms for each field.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Combine all the fields into one custom field."},{"label":"B","text":"Map the field display names as business terms."},{"label":"C","text":"Add the schema names as business terms."},{"label":"D","text":"Create new business terms for each field."}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Map the field display names as business terms.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Map the field display names as business terms.\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Copilot controls work more effectively when business-friendly\nterminology is mapped to Dataverse fields. Using the field display names as business terms allows Copilot to\nbetter understand non-standard terminology and custom columns in the Lead table while minimizing\nadministrative effort. Display names are already user-friendly and aligned with business language, making\nthem the ideal choice for improving summarization and AI understanding.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Combine all the fields into one custom field.\nThis is incorrect because combining multiple fields into a single custom field would reduce data structure\nquality and make lead management and summarization less efficient.\nC. Add the schema names as business terms.\nThis is incorrect because schema names are typically technical and not business-friendly, making them less\neffective for Copilot summarization and natural language understanding.\nD. Create new business terms for each field.\nThis is incorrect because creating entirely new business terms for every field introduces unnecessary\nadministrative overhead and does not minimize effort.","sourcePages":[58,59],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":55,"stem":"A company plans to deploy an AI-based customer service app that will autonomously manage\ninteractions, escalate complex cases, and learn from historical ticket data.\nYou need to perform a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis of the app deployment. The solution must\nensure that the analysis is accurate.\nWhat should you do first?","rawQuestion":"A company plans to deploy an AI-based customer service app that will autonomously manage\ninteractions, escalate complex cases, and learn from historical ticket data.\nYou need to perform a return on AI investment (ROAI) analysis of the app deployment. The solution must\nensure that the analysis is accurate.\nWhat should you do first?\nA. Establish the AI performance metrics.\nB. Conduct an AI market benchmarking study.\nC. Model the customer experience.\nD. Identify and quantify all the development, deployment, and operating costs.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Establish the AI performance metrics."},{"label":"B","text":"Conduct an AI market benchmarking study."},{"label":"C","text":"Model the customer experience."},{"label":"D","text":"Identify and quantify all the development, deployment, and operating costs."}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Identify and quantify all the development, deployment, and operating costs.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Identify and quantify all the development, deployment, and operating costs.\nThis option is correct because an accurate Return on AI Investment (ROAI) analysis must begin with\nestablishing the full cost baseline of the AI solution. This includes development costs, deployment expenses,\ninfrastructure, licensing, operational costs, maintenance, monitoring, and ongoing AI model usage expenses.\nWithout understanding the total investment, it is impossible to accurately calculate ROI, cost savings,\nproductivity gains, or business value generated by the AI deployment.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Establish the AI performance metrics.\nThis is incorrect because performance metrics are important for measuring effectiveness after deployment,\nbut ROAI analysis must first establish the financial investment baseline.\nB. Conduct an AI market benchmarking study.\nThis is incorrect because benchmarking may provide industry comparisons, but it is not the first step\nrequired for calculating the organization's actual AI return on investment.\nC. Model the customer experience.\nThis is incorrect because customer experience modeling helps evaluate business impact, but it does not\nestablish the foundational cost inputs required for accurate ROAI calculations.","sourcePages":[59,60],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":56,"stem":"A company has an AI solution that uses a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent. You need to monitor the\nagent's performance.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Monitor the agent's telemetry in near-real-time (NRT).\n• Download transcripts of full conversations.\n• Monitor the agent's usage and performance.\nWhat should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct\nrequirements. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI solution that uses a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent. You need to monitor the\nagent's performance.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Monitor the agent's telemetry in near-real-time (NRT).\n• Download transcripts of full conversations.\n• Monitor the agent's usage and performance.\nWhat should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct\nrequirements. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Application Insights\nCopilot Studio — Download transcripts\nCopilot Studio — Usage and performance","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nApplication Insights\nThis option is correct because Application Insights provides near-real-time telemetry monitoring for Microsoft\nCopilot Studio agents. It captures live operational data such as requests, dependencies, failures, response\ntimes, and diagnostic telemetry. This enables organizations to monitor agent behavior and performance with\nminimal delay.\nCopilot Studio — Download transcripts\nThis option is correct because Copilot Studio provides built-in analytics and transcript export capabilities.\nAdministrators can directly download full conversation transcripts from the Copilot Studio portal without\nrequiring additional infrastructure.\nCopilot Studio — Usage and performance\nThis option is correct because Copilot Studio contains native analytics dashboards that provide usage and\nperformance metrics such as engagement rates, escalation rates, session outcomes, resolution rates,\nabandonment rates, and customer satisfaction insights.\nIncorrect Answers:\nLog Analytics\nThis option is incorrect for this scenario because although Log Analytics can store and query telemetry data,\nit is not the primary built-in mechanism used for downloading full conversation transcripts from Copilot\nStudio.\nMicrosoft Power Apps\nThis option is incorrect because Power Apps is used for application development and integration rather than\nCopilot Studio telemetry monitoring or transcript analysis.","sourcePages":[60,61],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":57,"stem":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that will analyze historical business data to\npredict customer behavior.\nThe data is currently stored in an Azure SQL database, flat files, APIs, and logs.\nYou need to organize the data into a format that can be used as a knowledge source in Copilot Studio.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","rawQuestion":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that will analyze historical business data to\npredict customer behavior.\nThe data is currently stored in an Azure SQL database, flat files, APIs, and logs.\nYou need to organize the data into a format that can be used as a knowledge source in Copilot Studio.\nWhat should you include in the solution?\nA. Azure AI Search\nB. Azure Data Lake Storage\nC. Azure Cosmos DB\nD. Azure Translator in Foundry Tools","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Azure AI Search"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure Data Lake Storage"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure Cosmos DB"},{"label":"D","text":"Azure Translator in Foundry Tools"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Azure AI Search","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nA. Azure AI Search\nThis option is correct because Azure AI Search is designed to ingest, index, organize, and retrieve data from\nmultiple heterogeneous sources such as Azure SQL databases, flat files, APIs, and logs. It enables the data to\nbe transformed into a searchable and structured knowledge source that can be used effectively by Microsoft\nCopilot Studio for grounding, retrieval, and AI-driven responses.\nAzure AI Search is commonly used in Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architectures to provide\nenterprise knowledge grounding for Copilot and generative AI solutions.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Azure Data Lake Storage\nThis option is incorrect because Azure Data Lake Storage is primarily a scalable storage platform for\nstructured and unstructured data. It stores data but does not provide intelligent indexing, search, or retrieval\ncapabilities required for Copilot Studio knowledge grounding.\nC. Azure Cosmos DB\nThis option is incorrect because Cosmos DB is a globally distributed NoSQL database service and is not\nintended to organize multi-source enterprise knowledge for AI retrieval scenarios.\nD. Azure Translator in Foundry Tools\nThis option is incorrect because Azure Translator is used for language translation services and has no role in\norganizing enterprise knowledge sources for predictive AI or Copilot Studio grounding.","sourcePages":[61,62],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":58,"stem":"You need to recommend a Microsoft Power Platform business solution that consolidates data from\nmultiple internal and external data sources.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Provide the data as a centralized source for multiple AI systems, including Microsoft Copilot Studio\nagents, Dynamics 365 applications, and external AI models.\n• Support built-in data classification and protection policies. Provide data for grounding and analytics.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"You need to recommend a Microsoft Power Platform business solution that consolidates data from\nmultiple internal and external data sources.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Provide the data as a centralized source for multiple AI systems, including Microsoft Copilot Studio\nagents, Dynamics 365 applications, and external AI models.\n• Support built-in data classification and protection policies. Provide data for grounding and analytics.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Microsoft Dataverse\nB. Azure Data Lake Storage\nC. a Microsoft Power BI semantic model\nD. Azure Cosmos DB","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Dataverse"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure Data Lake Storage"},{"label":"C","text":"a Microsoft Power BI semantic model"},{"label":"D","text":"Azure Cosmos DB"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Microsoft Dataverse","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nA. Microsoft Dataverse\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Dataverse provides a centralized, secure, and scalable business data\nplatform for Microsoft Power Platform, Dynamics 365, Microsoft Copilot Studio agents, and external AI\nsystems. Dataverse supports data consolidation from multiple internal and external sources while providing\nbuilt-in security, governance, data classification, role-based access control, and protection policies.\nDataverse is also optimized for AI grounding, analytics, and integration with Copilot and Dynamics 365\napplications, making it the ideal centralized business data source for enterprise AI solutions.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Azure Data Lake Storage\nThis option is incorrect because Azure Data Lake Storage is primarily a large-scale storage platform and does\nnot provide the native business application integration, governance model, and built-in Power Platform data\nprotection capabilities offered by Dataverse.\nC. a Microsoft Power BI semantic model\nThis option is incorrect because a Power BI semantic model is designed for reporting and analytics\nconsumption rather than serving as a centralized operational data platform for multiple AI systems.\nD. Azure Cosmos DB\nThis option is incorrect because Cosmos DB is a globally distributed NoSQL database service but does not\nprovide the built-in Power Platform governance, business data modeling, and Copilot integration capabilities\nrequired in the scenario.","sourcePages":[62,63],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":59,"stem":"You need to design a shared prompt library that will be used across multiple business units.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure consistent AI responses with reusable formats.\n• Support governance and version control.\n• Minimize administrative effort.\n• Minimize ongoing costs.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"You need to design a shared prompt library that will be used across multiple business units.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure consistent AI responses with reusable formats.\n• Support governance and version control.\n• Minimize administrative effort.\n• Minimize ongoing costs.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Define standardized prompt templates\nStore prompts in a Git repository","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nDefine standardized prompt templates\nThis is correct because standardized prompt templates ensure that AI systems across multiple business units\ngenerate consistent responses by using reusable and governed prompt structures. This minimizes variation\nin AI outputs and reduces administrative overhead.\nStore prompts in a Git repository\nThis is correct because Git repositories provide built-in version control, change tracking, collaboration\nmanagement, rollback capability, and governance support. This approach also minimizes ongoing operational\ncosts because Git-based repositories are lightweight and widely adopted for centralized configuration\nmanagement.\nIncorrect Answers:\nDelegate department-specific prompt templates\nThis is incorrect because allowing departments to independently manage prompts can reduce response\nconsistency and increase governance complexity.\nMaintain a prompt history\nThis is incorrect because prompt history alone does not provide full governance, structured version control,\ncollaboration workflows, or rollback capabilities.\nCategorize prompts by business function\nThis is incorrect because categorization improves organization but does not directly address governance or\nversion control requirements.","sourcePages":[63,64],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":60,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft 365 tenant in Canada and multiple Microsoft Power Platform environments\nin Canada and the United States.\nThe company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to the Canadian environment that will use:\n• Microsoft Dataverse data stored in Canada\n• A connector that connects to an Azure OpenAI instance in the United States\nYou need to ensure that the agent adheres to data residency and data movement policies before being\ndeployed.\nWhat should you do?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft 365 tenant in Canada and multiple Microsoft Power Platform environments\nin Canada and the United States.\nThe company plans to deploy a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent to the Canadian environment that will use:\n• Microsoft Dataverse data stored in Canada\n• A connector that connects to an Azure OpenAI instance in the United States\nYou need to ensure that the agent adheres to data residency and data movement policies before being\ndeployed.\nWhat should you do?\nA. Ensure that the data processed by Azure OpenAI is stored in the United States.\nB. From the Microsoft Purview portal, validate the Data loss prevention settings.\nC. Migrate the tenant to the United States.\nD. Ensure that cross-region data movement is enabled for the Canadian environment and connector\ndependencies.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Ensure that the data processed by Azure OpenAI is stored in the United States."},{"label":"B","text":"From the Microsoft Purview portal, validate the Data loss prevention settings."},{"label":"C","text":"Migrate the tenant to the United States."},{"label":"D","text":"Ensure that cross-region data movement is enabled for the Canadian environment and connector dependencies."}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Ensure that cross-region data movement is enabled for the Canadian environment and connector\ndependencies.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nD. Ensure that cross-region data movement is enabled for the Canadian environment and connector\ndependencies.\nThis option is correct because the Microsoft Copilot Studio agent is deployed in a Canadian environment\nwhile using an Azure OpenAI connector hosted in the United States. This creates a cross-region data\nmovement scenario. To comply with data residency and data movement policies, cross-region data\nmovement must be explicitly enabled for the environment and the associated connector dependencies before\ndeployment.\nThis ensures that the Power Platform environment can legally and operationally exchange data with services\nhosted in another geographic region.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Ensure that the data processed by Azure OpenAI is stored in the United States.\nThis is incorrect because simply storing processed data in the United States does not address the\nrequirement to validate and permit cross-region data movement between Canada and the United States.\nB. From the Microsoft Purview portal, validate the Data loss prevention settings.\nThis is incorrect because Data Loss Prevention policies help govern connector usage and data exposure but\ndo not specifically enable or authorize cross-region data movement for Copilot Studio environments.\nC. Migrate the tenant to the United States.\nThis is incorrect because migrating the tenant is unnecessary and does not align with the requirement to\nmaintain Canadian data residency while using a U.S.-based Azure OpenAI connector.","sourcePages":[64,65],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":61,"stem":"A company has an AI agent that automates the review of customer feedback stored in a cloud database.\nYou plan to generate monthly reports from the agent's output to provide insights into customer sentiment\nand guide product development and marketing.\nYou need to ensure that the data ingested by the agent is clean and suitable for the intended use.\nWhat should you do to prepare the data?","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI agent that automates the review of customer feedback stored in a cloud database.\nYou plan to generate monthly reports from the agent's output to provide insights into customer sentiment\nand guide product development and marketing.\nYou need to ensure that the data ingested by the agent is clean and suitable for the intended use.\nWhat should you do to prepare the data?\nA. Create a workflow in Microsoft Power Automate.\nB. Identify and address biased data.\nC. Create an agent flow in Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nD. Sort the database by customer last name.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Create a workflow in Microsoft Power Automate."},{"label":"B","text":"Identify and address biased data."},{"label":"C","text":"Create an agent flow in Microsoft Copilot Studio."},{"label":"D","text":"Sort the database by customer last name."}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Identify and address biased data.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. Identify and address biased data.\nThis option is correct because preparing AI training and ingestion data requires ensuring that the data is\naccurate, representative, unbiased, and suitable for the intended business purpose. Since the AI agent\nanalyzes customer feedback and generates sentiment-based insights that influence product development and\nmarketing decisions, biased or unbalanced data could lead to misleading conclusions and poor business\noutcomes.\nIdentifying and correcting biased data is a critical part of responsible AI data preparation and helps improve\nthe reliability and fairness of generated insights.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Create a workflow in Microsoft Power Automate.\nThis is incorrect because Power Automate workflows help automate processes but do not clean or validate\ndata quality for AI ingestion.\nC. Create an agent flow in Microsoft Copilot Studio.\nThis is incorrect because agent flows manage conversational or orchestration logic rather than preparing or\ncleansing source data.\nD. Sort the database by customer last name.\nThis is incorrect because sorting data does not improve data quality, remove bias, or ensure suitability for AI\nanalysis.","sourcePages":[65,66],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":62,"stem":"You need to recommend a solution to integrate a Microsoft Copilot agent with a Microsoft Dynamics\n365 Contact Center chat channel.\nThe agent must respond to customer questions and hand off the conversation to a live customer service\nrepresentative when the customer requests an escalation.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"You need to recommend a solution to integrate a Microsoft Copilot agent with a Microsoft Dynamics\n365 Contact Center chat channel.\nThe agent must respond to customer questions and hand off the conversation to a live customer service\nrepresentative when the customer requests an escalation.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Build an agent flow.\nB. Configure the Conversation Start topic.\nC. Configure a skill.\nD. Call a Microsoft Power Automate connector.\nE. Configure the Escalate topic.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Build an agent flow."},{"label":"B","text":"Configure the Conversation Start topic."},{"label":"C","text":"Configure a skill."},{"label":"D","text":"Call a Microsoft Power Automate connector."},{"label":"E","text":"Configure the Escalate topic."}],"correct":["E"],"answer":"E. Configure the Escalate topic.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nE. Configure the Escalate topic.\nThis option is correct because the Escalate topic in Microsoft Copilot Studio is specifically designed to\ntransfer conversations from the Copilot agent to a live customer service representative in Dynamics 365\nContact Center. It supports escalation workflows when a customer explicitly requests human assistance or\nwhen the agent cannot resolve the issue.\nThis directly satisfies the requirement for both AI-driven responses and seamless handoff to a live agent.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Build an agent flow.\nThis is incorrect because agent flows help orchestrate conversation logic and automation but are not\nspecifically responsible for live-agent escalation handling.\nB. Configure the Conversation Start topic.\nThis is incorrect because the Conversation Start topic controls initial interaction behavior rather than\nescalation and handoff functionality.\nC. Configure a skill.\nThis is incorrect because skills extend bot capabilities but are not used for transferring conversations to live\ncustomer service representatives.\nD. Call a Microsoft Power Automate connector.\nThis is incorrect because Power Automate connectors can integrate services and automate workflows, but\nthey are not the native mechanism for Contact Center live-agent escalation.","sourcePages":[66,67],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":63,"stem":"You are designing a testing solution for a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that integrates with Microsoft\nDynamics 365 Customer Service and Dynamics 365 Sales.\nYou need to design end-to-end scenarios to test the agent's ability to perform the following actions:\n• Coordinate tasks and data interactions across both Dynamics 365 apps.\n• Interpret user input and provide contextually relevant outputs.\nWhich test scenario and metric should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"You are designing a testing solution for a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that integrates with Microsoft\nDynamics 365 Customer Service and Dynamics 365 Sales.\nYou need to design end-to-end scenarios to test the agent's ability to perform the following actions:\n• Coordinate tasks and data interactions across both Dynamics 365 apps.\n• Interpret user input and provide contextually relevant outputs.\nWhich test scenario and metric should you include in the design?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Run task-based scenarios that involve both apps\nTrack the successful completion of cross-app tasks","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nRun task-based scenarios that involve both apps\nThis is correct because the agent must coordinate tasks and data interactions across both Dynamics 365\nCustomer Service and Dynamics 365 Sales. End-to-end testing should therefore validate real business\nworkflows that span both applications rather than isolated functionality.\nThis also helps verify whether the agent can correctly interpret user input and provide contextually relevant\noutputs across integrated systems.\nTrack the successful completion of cross-app tasks\nThis is correct because the most meaningful metric for end-to-end orchestration testing is whether\nworkflows and tasks that span both applications complete successfully. This directly measures the agent's\nability to coordinate data, actions, and contextual interactions across multiple Dynamics 365 applications.\nIncorrect Answers:\nIn each app, test isolated tasks without using workflows\nThis is incorrect because isolated testing does not validate cross-application orchestration or contextual\nworkflow execution.\nTest visual consistency across both apps\nThis is incorrect because the requirement focuses on AI task coordination and contextual relevance rather\nthan UI consistency.\nMeasure the initial prompt response time for each app\nThis is incorrect because response time alone does not validate successful orchestration or contextual\nworkflow completion.\nTrack the average click rate across both apps\nThis is incorrect because click rate is not a meaningful metric for validating AI-driven cross-application\nbusiness task execution.","sourcePages":[67,68],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":64,"stem":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels.\nYou need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance\ninsights for the agents.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company uses multiple Microsoft Copilot Studio agents across different channels.\nYou need to recommend a monitoring solution that provides comprehensive telemetry data and performance\ninsights for the agents.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Application Insights\nB. Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice\nC. Log Analytics\nD. Microsoft Purview","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Application Insights"},{"label":"B","text":"Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice"},{"label":"C","text":"Log Analytics"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Purview"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Application Insights","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nA. Application Insights\nThis option is correct because Application Insights provides comprehensive telemetry, diagnostics, usage\nanalytics, performance monitoring, dependency tracking, failures, and operational insights for Microsoft\nCopilot Studio agents across multiple channels.\nIt supports near-real-time monitoring and helps organizations analyze agent behavior, response performance,\nconversation flows, latency, failures, and overall operational health.\nApplication Insights is the primary Azure-native monitoring solution used for centralized telemetry collection\nand performance insights for AI applications and Copilot Studio agents.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice\nThis is incorrect because Customer Voice is designed for surveys, feedback collection, and customer\nsatisfaction analysis rather than telemetry and operational performance monitoring.\nC. Log Analytics\nThis is incorrect because Log Analytics is primarily used for querying and analyzing collected logs. While it\ncan complement monitoring solutions, Application Insights is the primary telemetry and performance\nmonitoring service for Copilot Studio agents.\nD. Microsoft Purview\nThis is incorrect because Microsoft Purview focuses on governance, compliance, data classification, and data\nlineage rather than operational telemetry and performance monitoring.","sourcePages":[68,69],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":65,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry agent that summarizes customer feedback and recommends\nproducts to customers. The agent references data from multiple knowledge sources.\nUsers report that the agent response time is slow.\nTelemetry data shows that the agent frequently reaches its token usage limit.\nYou need to recommend a solution to reduce token usage without degrading the quality of the generated\nresponses.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry agent that summarizes customer feedback and recommends\nproducts to customers. The agent references data from multiple knowledge sources.\nUsers report that the agent response time is slow.\nTelemetry data shows that the agent frequently reaches its token usage limit.\nYou need to recommend a solution to reduce token usage without degrading the quality of the generated\nresponses.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Chunk documents during indexing.\nB. Reduce the number of knowledge sources used by the agent.\nC. Reconfigure the prompts to limit the amount of retrieved content from the knowledge sources.\nD. Lower the maximum token usage limit for the responses.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Chunk documents during indexing."},{"label":"B","text":"Reduce the number of knowledge sources used by the agent."},{"label":"C","text":"Reconfigure the prompts to limit the amount of retrieved content from the knowledge sources."},{"label":"D","text":"Lower the maximum token usage limit for the responses."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Reconfigure the prompts to limit the amount of retrieved content from the knowledge sources.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nC. Reconfigure the prompts to limit the amount of retrieved content from the knowledge sources.\nThis option is correct because excessive retrieved grounding content from multiple knowledge sources\nsignificantly increases token consumption in Microsoft Foundry agents. By optimizing prompts and limiting\nthe amount of retrieved content passed to the model, the organization can reduce token usage while still\nmaintaining high-quality responses.\nThis approach improves efficiency without removing important knowledge sources or degrading response\nquality. It is a common optimization technique in Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architectures.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Chunk documents during indexing.\nThis is incorrect because document chunking helps improve retrieval granularity and search relevance, but by\nitself it does not directly control how much retrieved content is ultimately sent to the model during prompt\ngeneration.\nB. Reduce the number of knowledge sources used by the agent.\nThis is incorrect because removing knowledge sources may reduce response quality and completeness by\nlimiting available contextual information.\nD. Lower the maximum token usage limit for the responses.\nThis is incorrect because simply lowering token limits may truncate responses and reduce response quality\nwithout addressing the root cause of excessive grounding content retrieval.","sourcePages":[69,70],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":66,"stem":"A company has an ecommerce support portal that uses Microsoft Dataverse.\nYou are designing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for the portal. The agent must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• Respond with a default help message when the user input is unclear.\n• Initiate external processes, such as retrieving the order status, when users make specific requests.\nGenerative orchestration will be enabled for the solution. You need to recommend a feature for each\nrequirement.\nWhat should you recommend?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate features to the correct requirements. Each feature may be used once, more\nthan once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"A company has an ecommerce support portal that uses Microsoft Dataverse.\nYou are designing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for the portal. The agent must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• Respond with a default help message when the user input is unclear.\n• Initiate external processes, such as retrieving the order status, when users make specific requests.\nGenerative orchestration will be enabled for the solution. You need to recommend a feature for each\nrequirement.\nWhat should you recommend?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate features to the correct requirements. Each feature may be used once, more\nthan once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"The Fallback topic\nA tool (connector)","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nThe Fallback topic\nThis is correct because the Fallback topic in Microsoft Copilot Studio is triggered when the agent cannot\nconfidently understand or match the user's input. It is specifically designed to provide default help messages,\nclarification prompts, or recovery guidance when user input is unclear.\nA tool (connector)\nThis is correct because tools and connectors allow Copilot Studio agents to invoke external systems, APIs,\nworkflows, and business processes such as retrieving order status information or initiating backend\noperations.\nWith generative orchestration enabled, the agent can intelligently determine when to invoke the connector\nbased on user intent.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA trigger phrase\nThis is incorrect because trigger phrases are used to activate specific topics and are not designed for\nhandling unclear user input.\nA skill\nThis is incorrect because skills extend capabilities between bots but are not the primary mechanism for\ninitiating external business processes in Copilot Studio.\nThe Escalate topic\nThis is incorrect because the Escalate topic is used for transferring conversations to human agents rather\nthan providing fallback responses or invoking external processes.","sourcePages":[70,71],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":67,"stem":"A company has an AI business solution.\nYou need to extend the solution so that Microsoft 365 Copilot can invoke external logic hosted in Azure\nservices.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI business solution.\nYou need to extend the solution so that Microsoft 365 Copilot can invoke external logic hosted in Azure\nservices.\nWhat should you include in the solution?\nA. Microsoft Copilot Studio skills\nB. Microsoft Power Platform connectors\nC. custom engine agents","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Copilot Studio skills"},{"label":"B","text":"Microsoft Power Platform connectors"},{"label":"C","text":"custom engine agents"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Microsoft Power Platform connectors","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. Microsoft Power Platform connectors\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Power Platform connectors enable Microsoft 365 Copilot to securely\nconnect to external Azure-hosted services, APIs, workflows, and business logic. Connectors provide a low-\ncode integration mechanism that allows Copilot solutions to invoke external functionality hosted in Azure\nservices such as Azure Functions, Logic Apps, APIs, and other backend systems.\nThis is the standard integration approach for extending Microsoft 365 Copilot with external enterprise logic\nwhile minimizing development effort.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Microsoft Copilot Studio skills\nThis option is incorrect because skills are primarily used to extend conversational capabilities between bots\nand agents rather than directly integrating Microsoft 365 Copilot with external Azure-hosted business logic.\nC. custom engine agents\nThis option is incorrect because custom engine agents are not the standard Microsoft integration mechanism\nfor invoking external Azure services from Microsoft 365 Copilot.","sourcePages":[72],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":68,"stem":"A company processes invoices stored across multiple systems in multiple formats.\nYou need to implement an AI solution to automate the invoice processing. The solution must meet the\nfollowing requirements:\n• Automate multi-step invoice processing tasks, including document analysis, data validation, and\napproval routing.\n• Enable users to interact directly via Microsoft Teams to review and approve invoices.\n• Minimize development efforts to define and customize approval workflows.\nWhat should you include in the solution?","rawQuestion":"A company processes invoices stored across multiple systems in multiple formats.\nYou need to implement an AI solution to automate the invoice processing. The solution must meet the\nfollowing requirements:\n• Automate multi-step invoice processing tasks, including document analysis, data validation, and\napproval routing.\n• Enable users to interact directly via Microsoft Teams to review and approve invoices.\n• Minimize development efforts to define and customize approval workflows.\nWhat should you include in the solution?\nA. Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools and Azure Logic Apps\nB. a SharePoint agent\nC. Microsoft Copilot Studio and AI Builder\nD. Azure OpenAI and Azure Functions","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools and Azure Logic Apps"},{"label":"B","text":"a SharePoint agent"},{"label":"C","text":"Microsoft Copilot Studio and AI Builder"},{"label":"D","text":"Azure OpenAI and Azure Functions"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Microsoft Copilot Studio and AI Builder","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nC. Microsoft Copilot Studio and AI Builder\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Copilot Studio and AI Builder together provide a low-code AI\nautomation solution that can automate invoice processing workflows with minimal development effort.\nAI Builder can analyze invoices, extract document data, and validate invoice information from multiple\ndocument formats. Microsoft Copilot Studio can orchestrate multi-step workflows, integrate approval routing,\nand enable users to interact through Microsoft Teams for reviewing and approving invoices.\nThis combination directly satisfies all the requirements:\ndocument analysis,\ndata validation,\napproval routing,\nTeams-based interaction,\nand low-code workflow customization.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Azure Document Intelligence in Foundry Tools and Azure Logic Apps\nThis option is incorrect because although Document Intelligence and Logic Apps can automate invoice\nprocessing, they typically require more custom integration and workflow development effort compared to\nCopilot Studio and AI Builder.\nB. a SharePoint agent\nThis option is incorrect because a SharePoint agent is not designed for enterprise invoice processing\nautomation, document extraction, or approval workflow orchestration.\nD. Azure OpenAI and Azure Functions\nThis option is incorrect because Azure OpenAI and Azure Functions would require significant custom\ndevelopment effort and do not provide the low-code workflow and approval capabilities requested in the\nscenario.","sourcePages":[72,73],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":69,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for customer support.\nYou are reviewing and validating the following prompts:\n• A prompt that has instructions to \"help the customer as best you can\"\n• A prompt that helps retrieve product information from a knowledge base\nYou need to ensure that the agent delivers consistent and accurate responses.\nWhat should you do for each prompt?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent for customer support.\nYou are reviewing and validating the following prompts:\n• A prompt that has instructions to \"help the customer as best you can\"\n• A prompt that helps retrieve product information from a knowledge base\nYou need to ensure that the agent delivers consistent and accurate responses.\nWhat should you do for each prompt?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Rewrite the prompt with clear and task-specific instructions\nUse responses with only reference sources and limit the response scope","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nRewrite the prompt with clear and task-specific instructions\nThis is correct because vague prompts such as “help the customer as best you can” can produce\ninconsistent and unpredictable outputs. Clear, task-specific prompts improve response consistency, accuracy,\nand reliability by explicitly defining the expected behavior and response boundaries.\nUse responses with only reference sources and limit the response scope\nThis is correct because grounding responses strictly on approved knowledge base sources helps ensure\nfactual accuracy and reduces hallucinations. Limiting the response scope further improves consistency and\nprevents the model from generating unsupported information.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAdd filler words to make the prompt sound more natural and conversational\nThis is incorrect because filler words do not improve prompt precision or response consistency.\nKeep the prompt vague to enable model flexibility\nThis is incorrect because vague prompts increase variability and reduce response reliability.\nAdd several open-ended questions to give the model broader context\nThis is incorrect because overly broad prompts can increase irrelevant or inconsistent responses.\nRemove the knowledge source so that the model responds freely with general product information\nThis is incorrect because removing grounding sources increases the risk of hallucinations and inaccurate\nresponses.","sourcePages":[73,74],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":70,"stem":"A company has a portfolio of AI initiatives at different stages of development.\nYou need to recommend a structured approach to evaluating the return on AI investment (ROAI) across all\nthe initiatives. The solution must balance immediate results with long-term values and strategic innovations.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company has a portfolio of AI initiatives at different stages of development.\nYou need to recommend a structured approach to evaluating the return on AI investment (ROAI) across all\nthe initiatives. The solution must balance immediate results with long-term values and strategic innovations.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. a simple cost and benefit analysis\nB. a horizon-based framework\nC. the internal rate of return (IRR) function\nD. a prioritization grid","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"a simple cost and benefit analysis"},{"label":"B","text":"a horizon-based framework"},{"label":"C","text":"the internal rate of return (IRR) function"},{"label":"D","text":"a prioritization grid"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. a horizon-based framework","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. a horizon-based framework\nThis option is correct because a horizon-based framework is designed to evaluate initiatives across different\nstages of maturity and investment timelines. It helps organizations balance short-term operational value,\nmedium-term transformation goals, and long-term strategic innovation.\nSince the company has multiple AI initiatives at different stages of development, a horizon-based framework\nprovides a structured approach for evaluating return on AI investment across the entire portfolio while\nconsidering both immediate and future business value.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. a simple cost and benefit analysis\nThis option is incorrect because a simple cost-benefit analysis mainly evaluates immediate financial gains and\ndoes not effectively measure long-term innovation or strategic AI value.\nC. the internal rate of return (IRR) function\nThis option is incorrect because IRR is a financial calculation used to estimate profitability, but it does not\nprovide a comprehensive framework for evaluating AI initiatives across multiple time horizons and strategic\ngoals.\nD. a prioritization grid\nThis option is incorrect because a prioritization grid helps rank initiatives but does not provide a structured\nROAI methodology that balances short-term outcomes with long-term innovation.","sourcePages":[75],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":71,"stem":"A company has Microsoft Copilot Studio agents.\nThe company plans to deploy custom connectors across development, test and production environments.\nYou need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure consistency and prevent\ndirect editing in production.\nWhich two actions should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"A company has Microsoft Copilot Studio agents.\nThe company plans to deploy custom connectors across development, test and production environments.\nYou need to design an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure consistency and prevent\ndirect editing in production.\nWhich two actions should you include in the design?\nA. Deploy managed solutions to production.\nB. Deploy unmanaged solutions to production.\nC. Manually rebuild the agents in each environment.\nD. Move the agents between the environments by using data export and import.\nE. Include agents and connectors in a solution.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Deploy managed solutions to production."},{"label":"B","text":"Deploy unmanaged solutions to production."},{"label":"C","text":"Manually rebuild the agents in each environment."},{"label":"D","text":"Move the agents between the environments by using data export and import."},{"label":"E","text":"Include agents and connectors in a solution."}],"correct":["A","E"],"answer":"A. Deploy managed solutions to production.\nE. Include agents and connectors in a solution.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Deploy managed solutions to production\nThis option is correct because managed solutions are intended for production environments. They help\nprevent direct editing of components in production, enforce governance, and ensure controlled deployments\nthrough the ALM pipeline.\nE. Include agents and connectors in a solution\nThis option is correct because adding agents and custom connectors into a solution enables consistent\npackaging, deployment, versioning, and lifecycle management across development, test, and production\nenvironments.\nThis is the recommended ALM approach for Microsoft Copilot Studio and Power Platform components.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Deploy unmanaged solutions to production\nThis option is incorrect because unmanaged solutions allow direct editing in production, which violates the\nrequirement to prevent uncontrolled modifications.\nC. Manually rebuild the agents in each environment\nThis option is incorrect because manual rebuilding increases administrative effort, introduces\ninconsistencies, and does not support proper ALM governance.\nD. Move the agents between the environments by using data export and import\nThis option is incorrect because exporting and importing data does not provide structured ALM, version\ncontrol, or connector deployment consistency.","sourcePages":[75,76],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":72,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 finance and operations apps.\nThe company plans to use Microsoft Copilot in-app help and guidance to generate responses for internal\nbusiness processes.\nYou need to add an additional knowledge source for the business processes. The solution must NOT add new\ntopics to the Copilot agent for the finance and operations apps.\nWhich knowledge source should you add?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 finance and operations apps.\nThe company plans to use Microsoft Copilot in-app help and guidance to generate responses for internal\nbusiness processes.\nYou need to add an additional knowledge source for the business processes. The solution must NOT add new\ntopics to the Copilot agent for the finance and operations apps.\nWhich knowledge source should you add?\nA. Microsoft Dataverse\nB. a public website\nC. Azure AI Search\nD. a file upload","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Dataverse"},{"label":"B","text":"a public website"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure AI Search"},{"label":"D","text":"a file upload"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. a file upload","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nD. a file upload\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Copilot in-app help and guidance for Dynamics 365 finance and\noperations apps supports uploaded files as knowledge sources. Business process documents, operational\nmanuals, and internal guidance documents can be uploaded directly and used by Copilot to generate\ncontextual responses.\nThis approach satisfies the requirement to add additional knowledge sources without creating new Copilot\ntopics.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Microsoft Dataverse\nThis option is incorrect because Dataverse is primarily intended for structured business application data\nrather than document-based knowledge grounding for in-app help scenarios.\nB. a public website\nThis option is incorrect because the requirement focuses on internal business process guidance rather than\nexternally hosted public content.\nC. Azure AI Search\nThis option is incorrect because Azure AI Search is generally used for advanced enterprise retrieval\narchitectures and would add unnecessary complexity for this scenario.","sourcePages":[76,77],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":73,"stem":"You are designing a testing solution for Microsoft Copilot Studio agents.\nYou need to validate prompt engineering best practices to ensure that the agents generate accurate and\ncontextually relevant responses.\nWhich prompt validation techniques and metrics should you include in the solution?","rawQuestion":"You are designing a testing solution for Microsoft Copilot Studio agents.\nYou need to validate prompt engineering best practices to ensure that the agents generate accurate and\ncontextually relevant responses.\nWhich prompt validation techniques and metrics should you include in the solution?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Use prompts that have varied phrasing\nResponse relevance and accuracy","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nUse prompts that have varied phrasing\nThis option is correct because validating prompts with different wording and phrasing helps ensure that\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio agents can consistently interpret user intent and generate accurate, contextually\nrelevant responses across diverse conversational styles.\nTesting varied phrasing is a core prompt engineering best practice because real users ask the same question\nin many different ways.\nResponse relevance and accuracy\nThis option is correct because the primary goal of prompt validation is ensuring that responses are both\ncontextually appropriate and factually correct. Measuring relevance and accuracy directly evaluates whether\nthe prompt engineering strategy is successful.\nIncorrect Answers:\nExclude domain-specific terminology from the prompts\nThis option is incorrect because domain-specific terminology is often essential for accurate contextual\nunderstanding in enterprise AI scenarios.\nUse only simple, one-word prompts\nThis option is incorrect because real-world user interactions are conversational and contextual, not limited to\nisolated keywords.\nThe number of words generated per response\nThis option is incorrect because response length alone does not measure correctness or contextual quality.\nThe response generation time\nThis option is incorrect because although performance is important, the question specifically focuses on\nvalidating accurate and contextually relevant responses.","sourcePages":[77,78,79],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":74,"stem":"A company has multiple AI models that support generation of sales transactions.\nEach release of the models must be reviewed by a security and compliance team before being deployed to\nthe production environment. The security and compliance team must have access to prior versions to\nproperly determine potential exposures introduced.\nYou need to recommend a solution to evaluate the impact of each deployment to production. The solution\nmust enhance business continuity.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company has multiple AI models that support generation of sales transactions.\nEach release of the models must be reviewed by a security and compliance team before being deployed to\nthe production environment. The security and compliance team must have access to prior versions to\nproperly determine potential exposures introduced.\nYou need to recommend a solution to evaluate the impact of each deployment to production. The solution\nmust enhance business continuity.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Create a central model registry that uses version history.\nB. Establish a promotion process by using a quality gate.\nC. Implement version control for all the AI system components.\nD. Track model retirement schedules to prevent service disruptions.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Create a central model registry that uses version history."},{"label":"B","text":"Establish a promotion process by using a quality gate."},{"label":"C","text":"Implement version control for all the AI system components."},{"label":"D","text":"Track model retirement schedules to prevent service disruptions."}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Create a central model registry that uses version history.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nA. Create a central model registry that uses version history.\nThis option is correct because the scenario specifically requires the security and compliance team to review\nprior model versions before approving deployments to production.\nA centralized model registry with version history provides:\ntraceability,\nversion comparison,\ngovernance,\nauditability,\nand rollback support.\nIt enables the organization to evaluate how each model release differs from previous versions and helps\nidentify new risks or compliance exposures introduced in a release.\nA model registry also improves business continuity because previous approved versions can be restored if\nissues occur after deployment.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Establish a promotion process by using a quality gate.\nThis option is incorrect because quality gates help enforce approval workflows, but they do not provide\ncentralized version tracking and historical model comparison capabilities required in the scenario.\nC. Implement version control for all the AI system components.\nThis option is incorrect because although version control is important, the requirement specifically focuses\non reviewing prior model versions and evaluating deployment impact through centralized governance and\nmodel lifecycle visibility.\nD. Track model retirement schedules to prevent service disruptions.\nThis option is incorrect because retirement tracking focuses on lifecycle maintenance and decommissioning\nrather than deployment impact analysis and version comparison.","sourcePages":[79,80],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":75,"stem":"A company has an AI business solution that uses Microsoft Copilot Studio agents.\nYou need to recommend prompt best practices to improve the effectiveness of agent interactions.\nWhich two actions should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"A company has an AI business solution that uses Microsoft Copilot Studio agents.\nYou need to recommend prompt best practices to improve the effectiveness of agent interactions.\nWhich two actions should you include in the recommendation?\nA. Track the duration of the average user session.\nB. Analyze the prompt length distribution.\nC. Regularly test and refine the prompts based on user input.\nD. Use clear and specific instructions in the prompts.\nE. Measure system resource usage during prompt processing.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Track the duration of the average user session."},{"label":"B","text":"Analyze the prompt length distribution."},{"label":"C","text":"Regularly test and refine the prompts based on user input."},{"label":"D","text":"Use clear and specific instructions in the prompts."},{"label":"E","text":"Measure system resource usage during prompt processing."}],"correct":["C","D"],"answer":"C. Regularly test and refine the prompts based on user input.\nD. Use clear and specific instructions in the prompts.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Regularly test and refine the prompts based on user input\nThis option is correct because prompt engineering is an iterative process. Continuously testing and refining\nprompts based on real user interactions helps improve response quality, contextual accuracy, and\nconversational effectiveness over time.\nD. Use clear and specific instructions in the prompts\nThis option is correct because clear and precise prompts reduce ambiguity and help Microsoft Copilot Studio\nagents generate more accurate, relevant, and consistent responses.\nSpecific instructions are a fundamental prompt engineering best practice.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Track the duration of the average user session\nThis option is incorrect because session duration is an analytics metric and does not directly improve prompt\neffectiveness.\nB. Analyze the prompt length distribution\nThis option is incorrect because prompt length alone does not determine prompt quality or interaction\neffectiveness.\nE. Measure system resource usage during prompt processing\nThis option is incorrect because resource usage measures infrastructure performance rather than prompt\nengineering quality.","sourcePages":[80,81],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":76,"stem":"A company plans to implement an AI business solution for a consumer goods company.\nYou need to create agents that meet the following requirements:\n• Orchestrate the sales order fulfillment and shipping of goods to customers.\n• Analyze historical data and trends to replenish stock.\nWhich type of agent should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate agent types to\nthe correct requirements. Each agent type may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"A company plans to implement an AI business solution for a consumer goods company.\nYou need to create agents that meet the following requirements:\n• Orchestrate the sales order fulfillment and shipping of goods to customers.\n• Analyze historical data and trends to replenish stock.\nWhich type of agent should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate agent types to\nthe correct requirements. Each agent type may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Autonomous","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nAutonomous\nThis is correct for orchestrating sales order fulfillment and shipping because autonomous agents are\ndesigned to independently coordinate complex, multi-step business workflows across multiple systems.\nSales order fulfillment and shipping involve:\ninventory validation,\npayment processing,\nshipment coordination,\nlogistics integration,\nstatus tracking,\nand exception handling.\nThese processes require dynamic decision-making and workflow orchestration capabilities, which are core\ncharacteristics of autonomous agents.\nTask\nThis is correct for analyzing historical data and trends because this requirement represents a focused and\nwell-defined business function.\nThe agent needs to:\nanalyze historical sales and inventory data,\nidentify trends,\nand generate replenishment insights or recommendations.\nTask agents are optimized for structured, bounded operations with a specific objective and predictable\noutput.\nIncorrect Answer:\nPrompt-and-response\nThis option is incorrect because prompt-and-response agents are primarily intended for conversational\ninteractions and user-driven Q&A scenarios.\nThey do not independently orchestrate enterprise workflows or execute structured analytical business\nprocesses.","sourcePages":[81,82],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":77,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform environment that contains Microsoft Dataverse data.\nYou create a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 that processes the Dataverse data. You discover\nthat Agent1 fails to return relevant or accurate results.\nYou need to improve the quality and reliability of data grounding.\nWhat should you do?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Power Platform environment that contains Microsoft Dataverse data.\nYou create a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent named Agent1 that processes the Dataverse data. You discover\nthat Agent1 fails to return relevant or accurate results.\nYou need to improve the quality and reliability of data grounding.\nWhat should you do?\nA. Retrain Agent1.\nB. Verify and cleanse the Dataverse data.\nC. Use an adaptive card in Agent1.\nD. Add example user inputs to the training data of Agent1.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrain Agent1."},{"label":"B","text":"Verify and cleanse the Dataverse data."},{"label":"C","text":"Use an adaptive card in Agent1."},{"label":"D","text":"Add example user inputs to the training data of Agent1."}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Verify and cleanse the Dataverse data.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. Verify and cleanse the Dataverse data.\nThis option is correct because grounding quality depends heavily on the quality, consistency, completeness,\nand accuracy of the source data.\nIf Agent1 is returning irrelevant or inaccurate responses while processing Microsoft Dataverse data, the most\neffective solution is to validate and cleanse the underlying data.\nData cleansing improves:\ndata consistency,\naccuracy,\ncompleteness,\nstandardization,\nand relevance.\nBetter source data directly improves grounding quality and the reliability of AI-generated responses.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Retrain Agent1\nThis option is incorrect because Copilot Studio agents primarily rely on grounding and orchestration rather\nthan traditional model retraining. Poor grounding results are usually caused by poor-quality source data\nrather than insufficient training.\nC. Use an adaptive card in Agent1\nThis option is incorrect because adaptive cards improve the user interface and interaction experience but do\nnot improve grounding quality or response accuracy.\nD. Add example user inputs to the training data of Agent1\nThis option is incorrect because example inputs may help conversational understanding, but they do not\nresolve inaccurate or unreliable grounding caused by poor Dataverse data quality.","sourcePages":[82,83],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":78,"stem":"You are designing an AI business solution that contains the following components:\n• A Microsoft Power Automate workflow\n• A Microsoft Copilot Studio agent A Microsoft Dataverse database\n• A Microsoft Power Apps app\nAs part of the application lifecycle management (ALM) process, you plan to package the components, so that\nthey can be deployed to other environments as a group.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports versioning, dependencies, and deployments.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?","rawQuestion":"You are designing an AI business solution that contains the following components:\n• A Microsoft Power Automate workflow\n• A Microsoft Copilot Studio agent A Microsoft Dataverse database\n• A Microsoft Power Apps app\nAs part of the application lifecycle management (ALM) process, you plan to package the components, so that\nthey can be deployed to other environments as a group.\nYou need to recommend a solution that supports versioning, dependencies, and deployments.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nA. GitHub Actions\nB. Azure DevOps\nC. Microsoft Power Platform solutions","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"GitHub Actions"},{"label":"B","text":"Azure DevOps"},{"label":"C","text":"Microsoft Power Platform solutions"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Microsoft Power Platform solutions","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nC. Microsoft Power Platform solutions\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Power Platform solutions are specifically designed for application\nlifecycle management in Power Platform environments.\nSolutions allow organizations to package related components together, such as Power Automate workflows,\nCopilot Studio agents, Dataverse components, and Power Apps applications, so they can be deployed\nconsistently across environments.\nPower Platform solutions support versioning, dependency tracking, managed and unmanaged deployment\nmodels, and environment-to-environment deployments, which directly satisfies the ALM requirements in the\nscenario.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. GitHub Actions\nThis option is incorrect because GitHub Actions is mainly used for CI/CD automation and pipeline\norchestration. It is not the native packaging and dependency-management mechanism for Power Platform\ncomponents.\nB. Azure DevOps\nThis option is incorrect because Azure DevOps can automate deployments and manage pipelines, but the\nactual grouping, packaging, dependency handling, and transport of Power Platform components is handled\nthrough Power Platform solutions.","sourcePages":[83,84],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":79,"stem":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management.\nYou are designing an AI supply chain process that meets the following requirements:\n• Provides managers with AI-driven insights that surface key information from customer orders\n• Helps planners use AI to anticipate future product needs more accurately\nYou need to recommend which Microsoft Copilot features to include in the design.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"A company uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management.\nYou are designing an AI supply chain process that meets the following requirements:\n• Provides managers with AI-driven insights that surface key information from customer orders\n• Helps planners use AI to anticipate future product needs more accurately\nYou need to recommend which Microsoft Copilot features to include in the design.\nWhat should you recommend for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"AI Summaries with Copilot\nGenerative insights for Demand planning","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nAI Summaries with Copilot\nThis is correct because AI Summaries with Copilot helps managers quickly surface and understand key\ninformation from customer orders, operational records, and related business data. It provides AI-generated\nsummaries that improve visibility and decision-making efficiency.\nThis directly satisfies the requirement to provide AI-driven insights from customer orders.\nGenerative insights for Demand planning\nThis is correct because Generative insights for Demand planning uses AI-driven forecasting and analytical\ncapabilities to help planners anticipate future product demand more accurately.\nIt analyzes historical trends, demand signals, and planning data to improve forecasting and inventory\nplanning decisions.\nThis directly satisfies the requirement to anticipate future product needs.\nIncorrect Answers:\nThe Customer credit and collections workspace\nThis option is incorrect because it focuses on financial collections and customer credit management rather\nthan customer-order insights and demand forecasting.\nWorkload insights with Copilot\nThis option is incorrect because it focuses on operational workload visibility rather than summarizing\ncustomer-order information.\nMicrosoft Power BI\nThis option is incorrect because although Power BI provides analytics and visualization capabilities, the\nquestion specifically asks for Microsoft Copilot features.\nProduct information management\nThis option is incorrect because it manages product master data rather than forecasting future demand.\nThe Supplier Communications Agent\nThis option is incorrect because it focuses on supplier interactions and communications rather than AI-driven\ndemand forecasting.","sourcePages":[84,85],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":80,"stem":"You need to recommend a security solution for agents in a Microsoft Power Platform environment.\nThe agents must use only approved connectors and services. The solution must prevent the agents from\naccessing sensitive data.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"You need to recommend a security solution for agents in a Microsoft Power Platform environment.\nThe agents must use only approved connectors and services. The solution must prevent the agents from\naccessing sensitive data.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Configure Azure Monitor to capture connector activity logs.\nB. Enable a Microsoft Dataverse audit.\nC. Deploy data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform.\nD. Enable customer-managed keys in Microsoft Dataverse.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Configure Azure Monitor to capture connector activity logs."},{"label":"B","text":"Enable a Microsoft Dataverse audit."},{"label":"C","text":"Deploy data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform."},{"label":"D","text":"Enable customer-managed keys in Microsoft Dataverse."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Deploy data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nC. Deploy data loss prevention (DLP) policies in Power Platform.\nThis option is correct because DLP policies in Microsoft Power Platform are specifically designed to control\nwhich connectors and services can be used by applications, flows, and agents.\nDLP policies help organizations restrict the use of unapproved connectors, prevent data movement between\ntrusted and untrusted services, and protect sensitive information from unauthorized access or exposure.\nThis directly satisfies the requirement that agents must use only approved connectors and services while\npreventing access to sensitive data.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Configure Azure Monitor to capture connector activity logs\nThis option is incorrect because Azure Monitor provides monitoring and logging capabilities only. It does not\nenforce connector restrictions or block sensitive data access.\nB. Enable a Microsoft Dataverse audit\nThis option is incorrect because Dataverse auditing tracks activities and data changes, but it does not control\nwhich connectors or services agents are allowed to use.\nD. Enable customer-managed keys in Microsoft Dataverse\nThis option is incorrect because customer-managed keys help control encryption ownership and key\nmanagement, but they do not enforce connector governance or prevent data movement between services.","sourcePages":[86],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":81,"stem":"A company extends Copilot in Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service.\nYou need to recommend an automated application lifecycle management (ALM) process so that the Copilot\ncomponents can be safely developed, tested, and promoted to production.\nWhich two actions should you include in the ALM process?","rawQuestion":"A company extends Copilot in Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Service.\nYou need to recommend an automated application lifecycle management (ALM) process so that the Copilot\ncomponents can be safely developed, tested, and promoted to production.\nWhich two actions should you include in the ALM process?\nA. Use an unmanaged solution in production.\nB. Rebuild the agents in each environment.\nC. Use Microsoft Power Platform pipelines.\nD. Include the components in a solution.\nE. Store the agent transcripts in source control.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Use an unmanaged solution in production."},{"label":"B","text":"Rebuild the agents in each environment."},{"label":"C","text":"Use Microsoft Power Platform pipelines."},{"label":"D","text":"Include the components in a solution."},{"label":"E","text":"Store the agent transcripts in source control."}],"correct":["C","D"],"answer":"C. Use Microsoft Power Platform pipelines.\nD. Include the components in a solution.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Use Microsoft Power Platform pipelines\nThis option is correct because Power Platform pipelines provide a standardized and automated ALM\nmechanism for deploying components across development, test, and production environments.\nPipelines help automate:\nenvironment promotion,\ndeployment validation,\nsolution movement,\nand governance controls.\nThis directly supports safe development, testing, and production promotion of Copilot components.\nD. Include the components in a solution\nThis option is correct because Power Platform solutions are the foundational packaging mechanism for ALM\nin Microsoft Power Platform and Dynamics 365 environments.\nSolutions allow Copilot components to be grouped, versioned, dependency-tracked, and transported\nconsistently across environments.\nThis is required for proper lifecycle management and deployment automation.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Use an unmanaged solution in production\nThis option is incorrect because production environments should use managed solutions to prevent direct\nediting and maintain governance and deployment integrity.\nB. Rebuild the agents in each environment\nThis option is incorrect because rebuilding agents manually increases administrative effort, introduces\ninconsistencies, and bypasses proper ALM practices.\nE. Store the agent transcripts in source control\nThis option is incorrect because transcripts are operational conversation records and are not ALM\ndeployment artifacts.","sourcePages":[86,87],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":82,"stem":"You are designing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses a custom Microsoft Foundry model to\ngenerate responses.\nYou need to ensure that the agent can securely connect to and invoke the custom model during user\ninteractions.\nWhat should you include in the design?","rawQuestion":"You are designing a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses a custom Microsoft Foundry model to\ngenerate responses.\nYou need to ensure that the agent can securely connect to and invoke the custom model during user\ninteractions.\nWhat should you include in the design?\nA. Configure the agent to use classic orchestration.\nB. Create a connection to Microsoft Foundry in the agent.\nC. Add the Microsoft Foundry model as a Copilot Studio skill.\nD. Create a custom engine agent.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Configure the agent to use classic orchestration."},{"label":"B","text":"Create a connection to Microsoft Foundry in the agent."},{"label":"C","text":"Add the Microsoft Foundry model as a Copilot Studio skill."},{"label":"D","text":"Create a custom engine agent."}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Create a connection to Microsoft Foundry in the agent.","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nB. Create a connection to Microsoft Foundry in the agent.\nThis option is correct because Microsoft Copilot Studio agents securely invoke custom Microsoft Foundry\nmodels through configured connections. Creating a connection to Microsoft Foundry enables the agent to\nauthenticate, access, and use the custom model during runtime interactions.\nThis approach provides secure integration between Copilot Studio and Microsoft Foundry while supporting\ngoverned AI model access during user conversations.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Configure the agent to use classic orchestration\nThis option is incorrect because orchestration mode controls how the agent manages conversations and\ntopics. It does not establish secure connectivity to a Microsoft Foundry model.\nC. Add the Microsoft Foundry model as a Copilot Studio skill\nThis option is incorrect because skills are used to extend conversational capabilities between agents and\nbots, not for securely connecting to external AI models hosted in Microsoft Foundry.\nD. Create a custom engine agent\nThis option is incorrect because custom engine agents are not the standard integration mechanism for\nsecurely invoking Microsoft Foundry models from Copilot Studio.","sourcePages":[87,88],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":83,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio prompt-and-response agent.\nYou need to ensure that the agent meets the following requirements:\n• Provides effective and relevant responses\n• Provides conversational outcomes\nWhich metric should you use for each requirement?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio prompt-and-response agent.\nYou need to ensure that the agent meets the following requirements:\n• Provides effective and relevant responses\n• Provides conversational outcomes\nWhich metric should you use for each requirement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Generated answer rate and quality","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nGenerated answer rate and quality\nThis metric is used to evaluate how effectively the Microsoft Copilot Studio agent generates accurate,\nrelevant, and useful responses during user interactions. It measures the quality and success rate of AI-\ngenerated answers and directly aligns with the requirement to provide effective and relevant responses.\nTopics by outcome\nThis metric is used to analyze how conversations progress and conclude across different topics and\ninteraction paths. It helps determine whether conversations are successfully resolved, escalated, abandoned,\nor completed, which directly supports the requirement to provide conversational outcomes.\nIncorrect Answers:\nReactions\nThis option is incorrect because reactions primarily capture user feedback indicators such as likes or dislikes.\nWhile useful for sentiment analysis, they do not directly measure response relevance or conversational\neffectiveness.\nTool use\nThis option is incorrect because tool-use metrics focus on how frequently tools, connectors, or actions are\ninvoked during conversations. They do not measure the quality of responses or conversation outcomes.\nSatisfaction\nThis option is incorrect because satisfaction metrics measure user sentiment or CSAT-style feedback rather\nthan tracking whether conversational objectives or outcomes were achieved.","sourcePages":[88,89,90],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":84,"stem":"A company is designing a Microsoft Power Platform solution to reduce the manual steps of a business\nprocess by deploying an existing AI model.\nYou need to calculate the return on AI investment (ROAI) by identifying the metadata and telemetry of the\nsolution.\nWhat should you use?","rawQuestion":"A company is designing a Microsoft Power Platform solution to reduce the manual steps of a business\nprocess by deploying an existing AI model.\nYou need to calculate the return on AI investment (ROAI) by identifying the metadata and telemetry of the\nsolution.\nWhat should you use?\nA. Microsoft Power Platform admin center\nB. Success by Design\nC. the Business value toolkit\nD. Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Power Platform admin center"},{"label":"B","text":"Success by Design"},{"label":"C","text":"the Business value toolkit"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. the Business value toolkit","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nC. the Business value toolkit\nThis option is correct because the Business value toolkit is specifically designed to help organizations\nevaluate and measure return on AI investment (ROAI) and business outcomes for Microsoft Power Platform\nand AI solutions.\nThe toolkit helps identify and measure:\nbusiness value metrics,\ntelemetry,\nusage insights,\noperational impact,\nproductivity improvements,\nand financial outcomes.\nThis directly supports calculating ROAI for an AI-enabled Power Platform solution.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Microsoft Power Platform admin center\nThis option is incorrect because the Power Platform admin center is primarily used for environment\nadministration, governance, monitoring, and management. It is not specifically intended for structured ROAI\nevaluation.\nB. Success by Design\nThis option is incorrect because Success by Design provides architectural and implementation guidance for\nPower Platform projects, but it is not a toolkit for calculating ROAI through metadata and telemetry analysis.\nD. Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure\nThis option is incorrect because the Cloud Adoption Framework focuses on cloud adoption strategy,\ngovernance, and operational guidance rather than AI investment-return analysis for Power Platform solutions.","sourcePages":[90,91],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":85,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses generative AI to assist Microsoft Dynamics\n365 Customer Service representatives.\nThe agent currently exhibits a low resolution rate and a high escalation rate. You need to identify the issue.\nWhat should you use?","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Copilot Studio agent that uses generative AI to assist Microsoft Dynamics\n365 Customer Service representatives.\nThe agent currently exhibits a low resolution rate and a high escalation rate. You need to identify the issue.\nWhat should you use?\nA. the Agent dashboard of Dynamics 365 Customer Service historical analytics\nB. the Insights tab from the Search & intelligence settings of the Microsoft 365 admin center\nC. the Copilot hub in the Power Platform admin center\nD. the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"the Agent dashboard of Dynamics 365 Customer Service historical analytics"},{"label":"B","text":"the Insights tab from the Search & intelligence settings of the Microsoft 365 admin center"},{"label":"C","text":"the Copilot hub in the Power Platform admin center"},{"label":"D","text":"the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answer:\nD. the Analytics tab in Copilot Studio\nThis option is correct because the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio provides detailed conversational\nanalytics and performance insights for Copilot agents.\nIt includes metrics such as:\nresolution rate,\nescalation rate,\nconversation outcomes,\nsession analytics,\nuser engagement,\nand generated answer quality.\nSince the issue involves a low resolution rate and a high escalation rate, Copilot Studio analytics is the\nappropriate tool to identify and analyze the root cause.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. the Agent dashboard of Dynamics 365 Customer Service historical analytics\nThis option is incorrect because historical analytics in Dynamics 365 Customer Service focuses more on\ncustomer service operational reporting and agent performance rather than detailed Copilot Studio\nconversational analytics.\nB. the Insights tab from the Search & intelligence settings of the Microsoft 365 admin center\nThis option is incorrect because this area is related to Microsoft 365 search and intelligence features, not\nCopilot Studio conversational performance analysis.\nC. the Copilot hub in the Power Platform admin center\nThis option is incorrect because the Copilot hub mainly provides governance and administrative oversight\nrather than detailed agent conversation analytics and escalation diagnostics.","sourcePages":[91,92],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":86,"stem":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry project that uses a single agent and a single prompt to complete a\nseries of tasks.\nThe agent encounters the following issues:\n• It frequently produces incomplete results.\n• It struggles with domain-specific reasoning.\n• Agent response times are remarkably slow.\nYou need to recommend a solution to improve the overall performance and accuracy of the agent.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct\nrequirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"A company has a Microsoft Foundry project that uses a single agent and a single prompt to complete a\nseries of tasks.\nThe agent encounters the following issues:\n• It frequently produces incomplete results.\n• It struggles with domain-specific reasoning.\n• Agent response times are remarkably slow.\nYou need to recommend a solution to improve the overall performance and accuracy of the agent.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct\nrequirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"To improve performance: Move to a multi-agent architecture","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nTo improve performance: Move to a multi-agent architecture\nThe current solution uses a single agent and a single prompt for multiple tasks, which causes slow\nresponses and incomplete processing. A multi-agent architecture distributes responsibilities across\nspecialized agents, improving efficiency, scalability, and overall performance.\nTo improve accuracy: Add a grounding data source\nThe agent struggles with domain-specific reasoning, which indicates that it lacks relevant contextual\nknowledge. Adding a grounding data source improves the accuracy and reliability of responses by allowing\nthe agent to reference trusted enterprise or domain-specific data.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAdd a prebuilt connector\nPrebuilt connectors help integrate external systems and services, but they do not directly address reasoning\nquality, response accuracy, or overall agent performance.\nUpgrade to a larger generative AI model\nA larger model may improve reasoning slightly, but it typically increases latency and cost. It does not address\nthe architectural inefficiencies caused by using a single agent for all tasks.","sourcePages":[92,93],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":87,"stem":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Foundry agent.\nYou need to recommend an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure that the agent\nevaluates against baseline accuracy metrics before being deployed.\nWhat should you recommend?","rawQuestion":"A company plans to deploy a Microsoft Foundry agent.\nYou need to recommend an application lifecycle management (ALM) process to ensure that the agent\nevaluates against baseline accuracy metrics before being deployed.\nWhat should you recommend?\nA. Configure GitHub Actions for new agent versions.\nB. Deploy each new agent version directly to production.\nC. Use Observability in Foundry Control Plane with evaluation and drift monitoring.\nD. Enable Application Insights and use Azure Monitor.","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Configure GitHub Actions for new agent versions."},{"label":"B","text":"Deploy each new agent version directly to production."},{"label":"C","text":"Use Observability in Foundry Control Plane with evaluation and drift monitoring."},{"label":"D","text":"Enable Application Insights and use Azure Monitor."}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Use Observability in Foundry Control Plane with evaluation and drift monitoring.","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Use Observability in Foundry Control Plane with evaluation and drift monitoring.\nObservability in the Foundry Control Plane provides built-in evaluation and drift monitoring capabilities that\nhelp validate agent performance against baseline accuracy metrics before deployment. This supports a\nreliable ALM process by continuously tracking quality, consistency, and model behavior over time.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Configure GitHub Actions for new agent versions.\nGitHub Actions helps automate CI/CD workflows, but it does not by itself evaluate agent accuracy or monitor\ndrift against baseline metrics.\nB. Deploy each new agent version directly to production.\nDirect deployment to production bypasses validation and testing processes, increasing the risk of inaccurate\nor unstable agent behavior.\nD. Enable Application Insights and use Azure Monitor.\nApplication Insights and Azure Monitor focus on telemetry, logs, and operational monitoring. They do not\nprovide specialized AI evaluation and drift monitoring capabilities for Foundry agents.","sourcePages":[93],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":88,"stem":"A global manufacturing company wants to integrate AI across its operations. The CTO has asked you to\nlead the initiative.\nThe company has no existing AI governance framework and limited AI experience among its staff.\nWhich approach should you recommend as the first step?","rawQuestion":"A global manufacturing company wants to integrate AI across its operations. The CTO has asked you to\nlead the initiative.\nThe company has no existing AI governance framework and limited AI experience among its staff.\nWhich approach should you recommend as the first step?\nA. Deploy Copilot Studio agents to automate the most time-consuming business processes immediately\nB. Implement the AI adoption process from the Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure, starting with defining\nan AI strategy and establishing governance\nC. Build custom AI models in Microsoft Foundry to demonstrate the potential of AI to leadership\nD. Purchase prebuilt Dynamics 365 agents and deploy them across all departments simultaneously","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Deploy Copilot Studio agents to automate the most time-consuming business processes immediately"},{"label":"B","text":"Implement the AI adoption process from the Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure, starting with defining an AI strategy and establishing governance"},{"label":"C","text":"Build custom AI models in Microsoft Foundry to demonstrate the potential of AI to leadership"},{"label":"D","text":"Purchase prebuilt Dynamics 365 agents and deploy them across all departments simultaneously"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Implement the AI adoption process from the Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure, starting with defining\nan AI strategy and establishing governance","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Implement the AI adoption process from the Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure, starting with defining\nan AI strategy and establishing governance\nSince the company has no AI governance framework and limited AI experience, the first priority should be\nestablishing a structured AI adoption strategy and governance model. The AI adoption process in the Cloud\nAdoption Framework for Azure provides guidance for governance, risk management, organizational\nreadiness, and responsible AI practices before large-scale deployment.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Deploy Copilot Studio agents to automate the most time-consuming business processes immediately\nDeploying AI agents immediately without governance, strategy, or organizational readiness can lead to\nsecurity, compliance, and operational risks.\nC. Build custom AI models in Microsoft Foundry to demonstrate the potential of AI to leadership\nBuilding custom AI models is premature when the organization lacks governance processes and AI maturity.\nD. Purchase prebuilt Dynamics 365 agents and deploy them across all departments simultaneously\nLarge-scale deployment across all departments without governance and phased adoption increases\nimplementation risk and reduces the likelihood of successful adoption.","sourcePages":[93,94],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":89,"stem":"A financial services firm wants an AI-powered solution to analyse customer interactions and provide\nnext-best-action recommendations to relationship managers. The firm already uses Dynamics 365 Sales. The\nsolution must integrate with their proprietary risk scoring system hosted in Azure.\nWhich approach best balances time-to-value with the firm's specific requirements?","rawQuestion":"A financial services firm wants an AI-powered solution to analyse customer interactions and provide\nnext-best-action recommendations to relationship managers. The firm already uses Dynamics 365 Sales. The\nsolution must integrate with their proprietary risk scoring system hosted in Azure.\nWhich approach best balances time-to-value with the firm's specific requirements?\nA. Build a custom AI solution from scratch in Microsoft Foundry using proprietary models\nB. Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot with custom connectors to the risk scoring system\nC. Extend the existing Copilot for Sales in Dynamics 365 with custom connectors to the risk scoring system\nand configure business terms for the firm's domain\nD. Buy a third-party AI sales analytics platform and integrate it with Dynamics 365","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Build a custom AI solution from scratch in Microsoft Foundry using proprietary models"},{"label":"B","text":"Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot with custom connectors to the risk scoring system"},{"label":"C","text":"Extend the existing Copilot for Sales in Dynamics 365 with custom connectors to the risk scoring system and configure business terms for the firm's domain"},{"label":"D","text":"Buy a third-party AI sales analytics platform and integrate it with Dynamics 365"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Extend the existing Copilot for Sales in Dynamics 365 with custom connectors to the risk scoring\nsystem and configure business terms for the firm's domain","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Extend the existing Copilot for Sales in Dynamics 365 with custom connectors to the risk scoring\nsystem and configure business terms for the firm's domain\nThe company already uses Dynamics 365 Sales, so extending Copilot for Sales provides the fastest time-to-\nvalue while leveraging existing investments. Custom connectors allow integration with the proprietary Azure-\nhosted risk scoring system, and configuring business terms enables the AI solution to align with the firm's\nfinancial services domain and terminology.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Build a custom AI solution from scratch in Microsoft Foundry using proprietary models\nBuilding a completely custom solution increases development time, complexity, and cost, reducing time-to-\nvalue.\nB. Extend Microsoft 365 Copilot with custom connectors to the risk scoring system\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot is more focused on productivity scenarios and is less specialized for Dynamics 365\nSales workflows and CRM-driven recommendations.\nD. Buy a third-party AI sales analytics platform and integrate it with Dynamics 365\nA third-party platform introduces additional integration complexity and may not align as effectively with\nexisting Dynamics 365 capabilities.","sourcePages":[94,95],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":90,"stem":"You are designing a customer service agent in Copilot Studio for an insurance company. The agent\nneeds to handle policy enquiries, claims submissions, and general questions. When the agent cannot\nunderstand a customer's request, it should collect the customer's details and create a support ticket.\nHow should you design the topic architecture?","rawQuestion":"You are designing a customer service agent in Copilot Studio for an insurance company. The agent\nneeds to handle policy enquiries, claims submissions, and general questions. When the agent cannot\nunderstand a customer's request, it should collect the customer's details and create a support ticket.\nHow should you design the topic architecture?\nA. Create a single topic that handles all three functions through branching logic\nB. Create separate topics for policy enquiries, claims submissions, and general questions, with a fallback\ntopic that collects customer details and creates a support ticket\nC. Create separate topics without a fallback, relying on the agent to redirect users to the company website\nD. Create a single generative AI topic and allow the model to determine the correct response for all\nscenarios","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Create a single topic that handles all three functions through branching logic"},{"label":"B","text":"Create separate topics for policy enquiries, claims submissions, and general questions, with a fallback topic that collects customer details and creates a support ticket"},{"label":"C","text":"Create separate topics without a fallback, relying on the agent to redirect users to the company website"},{"label":"D","text":"Create a single generative AI topic and allow the model to determine the correct response for all scenarios"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Create separate topics for policy enquiries, claims submissions, and general questions, with a fallback\ntopic that collects customer details and creates a support ticket","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Create separate topics for policy enquiries, claims submissions, and general questions, with a fallback\ntopic that collects customer details and creates a support ticket\nUsing separate topics provides better organization, maintainability, and accuracy for different business\nscenarios. A fallback topic ensures that when the agent cannot understand the request, customer details are\ncollected and a support ticket is created, improving customer experience and operational continuity.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Create a single topic that handles all three functions through branching logic\nA single large topic becomes difficult to maintain, scale, and troubleshoot as complexity increases.\nC. Create separate topics without a fallback, relying on the agent to redirect users to the company website\nWithout a fallback topic, unresolved customer requests may lead to poor user experience and missed\nsupport cases.\nD. Create a single generative AI topic and allow the model to determine the correct response for all\nscenarios\nRelying entirely on generative AI reduces control and predictability for structured insurance workflows like\nclaims and policy handling.\n\nFabrikam Case Study\nBackground:\nFabrikam Inc. is a global consumer goods company that is undergoing a digital transformation initiative to\nmigrate its entire infrastructure to the Microsoft cloud. As a key element of this cloud migration, the company\nwill implement Microsoft Dynamics 365 Sales, moving away from the current on-premises proprietary\ntechnologies used by its business-to-business (B2B) sales team.\nAs part of the cloud migration, Fabrikam will adopt an AI-first approach to its business solutions and\nimplement AI solutions, wherever possible, to streamline operations.\nProblem Statements:\nFabrikam's infrastructure currently relies on various on-premises systems that require sales executives to\nuse corporate computers with physical keyboards to access business information during customer\ninteractions. Mobile phones cannot be used for these purposes, as the systems depend on keyboard input.\nAs a result, the sales executives spend a lot of time using keyboards to search for data on several disparate\nsystems and file servers, rather than focusing on the customers. This affects the customer experience.\nFabrikam stakeholders are concerned that users will be hesitant to adopt AI. If the AI initiatives are NOT\nadopted, cost savings will never be realized. Additionally, funding for future AI initiatives will depend on\ndemonstrating an increase in AI adoption month over month. As the AI agent initiative for the sales team will\nbe the first for Fabrikam, the rapid adoption of the agent is a high priority.\nPlanned Initiatives:\nGeneral:\nFabrikam management has prioritized AI-driven projects to improve efficiency, customer engagement, and\nresponsible AI adoption. The current application infrastructure is on-premises and must be migrated to the\ncloud to support the adoption of these technologies.\nInfrastructure Migration:\nFabrikam plans to migrate from its current on-premises infrastructure to a completely cloud-based topology;\nthis will include user authentication, the security framework, and, primarily, the adoption of the services by\nend users. All the data from the different systems will be consolidated into a single data source - a common\ndata model that will use a Microsoft Dataverse environment as a single source of truth (SSOT) for the sales\nteam.\nSales Cycle Enablement:\nTo achieve the company's objectives, Fabrikam intends to implement the following strategies to enhance the\nsales cycle:\n• Use low-code development to create a single AI agent that has Dataverse as its core component.\n• Ensure that sales managers can access unanswered correspondence from prospects and intervene\nas appropriate.\n• Replace the previous proprietary software with Dynamics 365 Sales to track sales cycles and\ncustomer interactions.\n• Have the sales executives use Dynamics 365 Sales to track interactions for open opportunities and\nsend follow-up communications to prospects.\n• Have the sales executives use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI agent when they have\nquestions about internal policies or customer data.\nRequirements:\nInfrastructure Migration:\nFabrikam has identified the following infrastructure migration requirements:\n• Azure must be used for all future infrastructure workloads.\n• The company must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for infrastructure migration to the\ncloud.\n• Any created AI agents must have their return on investment (ROI) calculated to ensure that the\nsolution will save the company money.\nSales Cycle Enablement -\nFabrikam has identified the following requirements for sales cycle enablement:\n• The final AI agent must follow Microsoft recommendations for a conversational user experience.\n• A designated checklist must be reviewed to ensure that the AI agent follows Microsoft deployment\nrecommendations for a compliant solution.\n• Detailed telemetry must be logged for the first created AI agent to help troubleshoot and optimize the\nagent during the initial AI agent adoption process.\n• Unexpected AI agent actions must end in an escalation to a live representative. For example, a sales\nexecutive must be rerouted to a representative if the agent cannot answer a question after two failed\nattempts.\n• The return on investment (ROI) of switching from the current process to the future process is\nrequired for stakeholder sign off.\n• The sales team must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with prospects more quickly and\nefficiently than currently.\n• Sales managers must report on the adoption of the AI agent to key Fabrikam stakeholders on a\nmonthly basis.\n• Any sensitive information, such as user IDs and names, shared via the AI agent must be tracked for\nfuture auditing.","sourcePages":[95,96,97,98,99],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":91,"stem":"Which tool should you recommend to address the sensitive information concerns in the sales process?","rawQuestion":"Which tool should you recommend to address the sensitive information concerns in the sales process?\nA. the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nB. Model Context Protocol (MCP)\nC. Application Insights\nD. Microsoft Foundry Tracing UI\nE. Monitoring in Microsoft Foundry","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio"},{"label":"B","text":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)"},{"label":"C","text":"Application Insights"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Foundry Tracing UI"},{"label":"E","text":"Monitoring in Microsoft Foundry"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. Microsoft Foundry Tracing UI","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. Microsoft Foundry Tracing UI\nThe case study states that sensitive information such as user IDs and names shared through the AI agent\nmust be tracked for future auditing. Microsoft Foundry Tracing UI provides detailed traceability and\nmonitoring of AI agent interactions, making it suitable for auditing sensitive information and investigating AI\nbehavior.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThe Analytics tab focuses on usage and performance metrics, not detailed auditing of sensitive information.\nB. Model Context Protocol (MCP)\nMCP standardizes communication between models and tools but does not provide auditing or sensitive data\ntracking capabilities.\nC. Application Insights\nApplication Insights captures telemetry and diagnostics but is not specifically designed for tracing sensitive\nAI interaction data for auditing.\nE. Monitoring in Microsoft Foundry\nMonitoring provides operational oversight and health metrics, but the Tracing UI is more appropriate for\ndetailed auditing and trace-level visibility.","sourcePages":[99,100],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":92,"stem":"Which components should you use to meet the sales cycle enablement requirements?","rawQuestion":"Which components should you use to meet the sales cycle enablement requirements?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio\nThe case study specifies that Fabrikam wants to use low-code development to create a single AI agent with\nDataverse as the core component. Microsoft Copilot Studio is the recommended low-code platform for\nbuilding conversational AI agents integrated with Dataverse and Dynamics 365.\na Fallback topic\nFabrikam requires that unexpected AI agent actions must escalate to a live representative after failed\nattempts. A Fallback topic is specifically designed to handle unrecognized or failed conversations and reroute\nusers appropriately.\nIncorrect Answers:\nMicrosoft Foundry\nMicrosoft Foundry is more suited for advanced AI development scenarios rather than low-code\nconversational agent creation.\nDynamics 365 Sales\nDynamics 365 Sales manages sales workflows and customer interactions but is not the primary AI agent\ncreation platform.\nthe Power Platform admin center\nThe Power Platform admin center is used for administration and governance, not agent development.\na custom connector\nCustom connectors integrate external systems but do not handle unexpected conversational flows.\nan event trigger\nEvent triggers initiate workflows but are not designed for escalation handling after failed AI interactions.\na REST API\nREST APIs enable communication between services but do not provide conversational fallback capabilities.","sourcePages":[100,101],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":93,"stem":"Which framework should you use for the infrastructure migration?","rawQuestion":"Which framework should you use for the infrastructure migration?\nA. Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure\nB. Success by Design\nC. Microsoft Power Platform Center of Excellence (CoE)\nD. Microsoft Power Platform Project Setup Wizard","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure"},{"label":"B","text":"Success by Design"},{"label":"C","text":"Microsoft Power Platform Center of Excellence (CoE)"},{"label":"D","text":"Microsoft Power Platform Project Setup Wizard"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure\nThe case study explicitly states that Fabrikam must follow Microsoft-recommended methodologies for\ninfrastructure migration to the cloud. The Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure provides proven\nguidance, best practices, governance, strategy, and migration methodologies for moving enterprise\ninfrastructure to Azure.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Success by Design\nSuccess by Design focuses on Dynamics 365 implementation guidance, not enterprise infrastructure\nmigration to Azure.\nC. Microsoft Power Platform Center of Excellence (CoE)\nThe CoE provides governance and best practices for Power Platform adoption, not cloud infrastructure\nmigration.\nD. Microsoft Power Platform Project Setup Wizard\nThe Project Setup Wizard helps configure Power Platform projects but is not a migration framework for Azure\ninfrastructure.","sourcePages":[102],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":94,"stem":"Which existing tool and data should you use to gather the required metrics for stakeholder signoff for\nthe AI agents?","rawQuestion":"Which existing tool and data should you use to gather the required metrics for stakeholder signoff for\nthe AI agents?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Copilot Studio\nThe Fabrikam case study states that stakeholder signoff requires calculating the return on investment (ROI)\nof moving from the current manual process to the future AI-assisted process. Microsoft Copilot Studio\nincludes capabilities that help organizations measure productivity improvements, automation impact, and\noperational efficiency gains delivered by AI agents. Since Fabrikam plans to build a low-code AI agent using\nMicrosoft technologies, Copilot Studio is the most appropriate existing tool to help gather and evaluate these\nmetrics.\nthe current time to complete the task today per instance\nTo accurately calculate ROI, Fabrikam needs a measurable baseline from the current process. The current\ntime required to complete each task instance provides a direct operational metric that can later be compared\nwith the AI-assisted workflow. This allows stakeholders to quantify productivity gains, efficiency\nimprovements, and time savings achieved through the AI agent implementation.\nIncorrect Answers:\nMicrosoft Foundry\nMicrosoft Foundry is primarily focused on AI application development and orchestration, not ROI estimation\nfor business process automation.\nAzure Resource Monitor (ARM)\nARM is used for Azure resource deployment and management and does not provide business productivity\nmetrics.\nDynamics 365 Sales\nDynamics 365 Sales manages customer and sales data but is not the primary tool for measuring AI solution\nROI.\nthe cumulative time spent on the task over the past year\nThis provides historical data, but it is less effective for measuring real-time process efficiency improvements\nper transaction.\nthe current cost to complete the tasks per instance\nCost data is useful, but operational time savings are the most direct and commonly used productivity metric\nfor AI ROI calculations.\nthe current cost of the Dynamics 365 Sales licenses\nLicense costs alone do not demonstrate efficiency improvements or business value from AI adoption.","sourcePages":[102,103,104],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":95,"stem":"Which framework should you use for the infrastructure migration?","rawQuestion":"Which framework should you use for the infrastructure migration?\nA. IT Helpdesk in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nB. AI agents in Microsoft Foundry\nC. Voice in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nD. AI chat in Microsoft Foundry","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"IT Helpdesk in Microsoft Copilot Studio"},{"label":"B","text":"AI agents in Microsoft Foundry"},{"label":"C","text":"Voice in Microsoft Copilot Studio"},{"label":"D","text":"AI chat in Microsoft Foundry"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. Voice in Microsoft Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. Voice in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThe Fabrikam case study states that sales executives must use handsfree headsets to interact with an AI\nagent when asking questions about internal policies or customer data. Voice capabilities in Microsoft Copilot\nStudio are specifically designed for conversational voice interactions, enabling users to communicate with AI\nagents handsfree. This directly supports the company's requirement to reduce dependency on keyboards and\nimprove the customer interaction experience.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. IT Helpdesk in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThe IT Helpdesk template is designed for IT support scenarios and does not specifically address voice-\nenabled sales interactions.\nB. AI agents in Microsoft Foundry\nMicrosoft Foundry supports advanced AI agent development but is not the primary low-code voice\ninteraction solution for sales executives.\nD. AI chat in Microsoft Foundry\nAI chat focuses on text-based conversational experiences rather than handsfree voice-enabled interactions.","sourcePages":[104,105],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":96,"stem":"Which framework should you use to meet the AI agent requirements for the sales cycle enablement?","rawQuestion":"Which framework should you use to meet the AI agent requirements for the sales cycle enablement?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Power Platform Well-Architected framework","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Power Platform Well-Architected framework\nThe Fabrikam case study requires that a designated checklist be reviewed to ensure the AI agent follows\nMicrosoft deployment recommendations for a compliant solution. The Microsoft Power Platform Well-\nArchitected framework provides best practices, design guidance, operational excellence principles, security\nrecommendations, and governance guidance for Power Platform and Copilot Studio solutions.\nSuccess by Design\nThe case study also requires that the AI agent follow Microsoft recommendations for conversational user\nexperiences. Success by Design provides guidance and design principles for creating effective, user-friendly,\nand scalable conversational experiences within Dynamics 365 and Power Platform solutions.\nIncorrect Answers:\nthe ALM Accelerator for Microsoft Power Platform\nThe ALM Accelerator supports deployment automation and lifecycle management but does not primarily\ndefine conversational UX or architectural best practices.\nMicrosoft Cloud Adoption Framework for Azure\nThis framework focuses on cloud adoption and infrastructure migration rather than conversational AI\nexperience design.","sourcePages":[105,106],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":97,"stem":"Which tool should you recommend to help secure funding for future AI agent development?","rawQuestion":"Which tool should you recommend to help secure funding for future AI agent development?\nA. Evaluations in Microsoft Foundry\nB. the Azure Cost Optimization workbook\nC. Azure Operator Insights\nD. the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nE. Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Evaluations in Microsoft Foundry"},{"label":"B","text":"the Azure Cost Optimization workbook"},{"label":"C","text":"Azure Operator Insights"},{"label":"D","text":"the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio"},{"label":"E","text":"Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. the Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThe Fabrikam case study states that funding for future AI initiatives depends on demonstrating increased AI\nadoption month over month. The Analytics tab in Microsoft Copilot Studio provides detailed usage metrics,\nadoption trends, engagement statistics, conversation success rates, and user interaction insights. These\nanalytics help sales managers report adoption metrics to stakeholders and justify continued investment in\nfuture AI agent development.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Evaluations in Microsoft Foundry\nEvaluations focus on assessing model quality and performance rather than measuring organizational\nadoption trends.\nB. the Azure Cost Optimization workbook\nThis workbook helps optimize Azure spending but does not measure AI agent adoption or business\nengagement.\nC. Azure Operator Insights\nAzure Operator Insights is designed for telecom and operational analytics scenarios, not AI adoption\nreporting.\nE. Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)\nDPO is a model tuning technique used to improve AI responses and is unrelated to adoption measurement or\nfunding justification.","sourcePages":[106,107],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":98,"stem":"Which tool should you use for the prospect communication requirements in Dynamics 365 Sales?","rawQuestion":"Which tool should you use for the prospect communication requirements in Dynamics 365 Sales?\nA. Azure AI Search\nB. Copilot email assist\nC. the Voice template Microsoft Copilot Studio\nD. Deep Research in Microsoft Foundry Agent Service","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Azure AI Search"},{"label":"B","text":"Copilot email assist"},{"label":"C","text":"the Voice template Microsoft Copilot Studio"},{"label":"D","text":"Deep Research in Microsoft Foundry Agent Service"}],"correct":["B"],"answer":"B. Copilot email assist","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. Copilot email assist\nThe Fabrikam case study states that sales executives must use Dynamics 365 Sales to correspond with\nprospects more quickly and efficiently. Copilot email assist in Dynamics 365 Sales helps users draft,\nsummarize, and generate contextual follow-up emails rapidly using AI. This directly improves communication\nspeed, productivity, and customer engagement throughout the sales cycle.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. Azure AI Search\nAzure AI Search is used for indexing and searching enterprise data, not for generating prospect\ncommunications.\nC. the Voice template Microsoft Copilot Studio\nThe Voice template supports voice-based conversational experiences but does not specifically optimize email\ncommunication with prospects.\nD. Deep Research in Microsoft Foundry Agent Service\nDeep Research is intended for advanced AI research and analysis tasks rather than sales communication\nassistance.\nContoso Case Study\nOverview:\nContoso Ltd. is a high-tech manufacturing company that uses Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance. Dynamics\n365 Supply Chain Management, and Dynamics 365 Commerce for its North American operations. The\ncompany designs and develops innovative products that have many patents and proprietary technologies.\nThe patents and engineering designs are closely guarded secrets.\nContoso executives want to integrate and adopt AI solutions to help scale the company in preparation for an\nanticipated period of rapid growth.\nThe company has multiple legal entities and Azure subscriptions that will be used in the adopted AI solutions.\nRequirements:\nAI Adoption:\nThe following executives will have specific responsibilities in the overall AI adoption:\n• Chief Technology Officer (CTO): Select one Dynamics 365 Finance, Dynamics 365 Supply Chain\nManagement or Dynamics 365 Commerce prebuilt AI agent and one custom Microsoft Copilot Studio\nAI agent to prioritize and deploy during the initial AI adoption phase.\n• Chief Information Officer (CIO): Ensure that appropriate security labels are assigned to the data used\nby the AI agents.\n• Chief Financial Officer (CFO): Analyze the return on investment (ROI) for the AI agents being\ndeployed.\n• Chief Information Security Officer (CISO): Discover and inventory AI resources for auditing.\n• Chief Executive Officer (CEO): Ensure that all solutions adhere to industry-standard responsible AI\npractices.\nAll AI initiatives and agents will have a detailed business use case, a defined audience profile, and an\nestimated ROI that will compare the cost savings of the current process against the estimated costs of using\nthe new AI solutions.\nThe company's research and development (R&D) department already has a custom Model Context Protocol\n(MCP) server that contains comprehensive product specifications and compliance data.\nPrebuilt AI Agent:\nThe CTO has NOT yet selected which prebuilt AI agent to use in Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management.\nThe CTO wants to view available agent templates to identify which agent will add the most business value.\nDepending on which high-priority AI agents are identified, its agent capabilities must be previewed in a\ndiscovery meeting with the relevant business operation stakeholders.\nCustom AI Agent:\nContoso has identified the following custom AI agent requirements:\n• The custom AI agent will use data from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management to answer\nquestions for the manufacturing team as a low-code solution.\n• The custom AI agent will be accessible from within Microsoft Teams.\n• The custom AI agent must be designed to eventually connect to other agents that can be selected\nbased on their description.\n• The topics used in the custom AI agent will be selected based NOT on a trigger phrase, but on a\ndescription of the purpose of the query, to make the interactions more conversational.\n• The custom AI agent must be able to answer questions about product specifications by using existing\ntechnologies. The product specifications are maintained by the R&D department.\n• The custom AI agent must be integrated with and accessible from Dynamics 365 Supply Chain\nManagement.\n• The custom AI agent must be able to use Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management business logic\nthat is stored outside of the application.\nAnalysis, Reporting, and Troubleshooting:\nContoso has identified the following analysis, reporting, and troubleshooting requirements:\n• The CISO will audit all the AI solutions monthly for compliance and security.\n• The CFO will analyze all the AI solutions quarterly to compare the estimated ROI against actual\nmeasured efficiencies and adoption. The CFO will use the Copilot Studio agent usage estimator to\nperform this analysis.\n• The CISO wants to identify how much sensitive data was accessed for a given AI agent run and who\naccessed the data. Too much sensitive data accessed by a single user might indicate a high security\nrisk.\n• The CTO wants to track user feedback on the quality of the AI agent responses during user\ninteractions with the agents. Consistently poor feedback will trigger an escalated reengineering\ndiscussion.\n• The CEO wants a quarterly assessment of all the required metrics for their specific responsibilities.\nThe tools used for the assessments must be Microsoft-recommended and must verify reliability,\ninterpretability, fairness, and compliance.\n• The CFO wants to identify how many interactions with the AI agents are abandoned on a given day as\ncompared to resolved conversations. Too many abandoned sessions might indicate that Copilot\nStudio credits are being used inefficiently by end users.","sourcePages":[107,108,109,110],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":99,"stem":"Which two components in the custom AI agent design should the CFO evaluate in the quarterly agent\nanalysis?","rawQuestion":"Which two components in the custom AI agent design should the CFO evaluate in the quarterly agent\nanalysis?\nA. the GPT models used for the agent\nB. the average characters in a chat message\nC. the agent orchestration method\nD. the average session time per agent","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"the GPT models used for the agent"},{"label":"B","text":"the average characters in a chat message"},{"label":"C","text":"the agent orchestration method"},{"label":"D","text":"the average session time per agent"}],"correct":["A","C"],"answer":"A. the GPT models used for the agent\nC. the agent orchestration method","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. the GPT models used for the agent\nThe GPT models selected for the AI agent directly affect operational cost, response quality, latency, and\noverall efficiency. Since the CFO is responsible for quarterly ROI analysis and comparing estimated savings\nagainst actual efficiencies, evaluating the models used is important to understand whether the AI solution is\ncost-effective and appropriately optimized for business value.\nC. the agent orchestration method\nThe orchestration method determines how the AI agent coordinates workflows, invokes tools, routes tasks,\nand manages interactions between systems or subagents. Different orchestration approaches can impact\ncompute consumption, scalability, performance, and operational efficiency. These factors directly influence\nROI and are therefore important for CFO-level quarterly analysis.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. the average characters in a chat message\nAverage message length is a low-level conversational metric and does not provide meaningful financial or\noperational insight for ROI evaluation.\nD. the average session time per agent\nSession duration may indicate engagement trends, but by itself it does not directly evaluate architectural\nefficiency, operational cost, or AI optimization effectiveness.","sourcePages":[110,111],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":100,"stem":"What should you include in the custom AI agent design to meet the R&D product specifications and\nthe compliance information requirements?","rawQuestion":"What should you include in the custom AI agent design to meet the R&D product specifications and\nthe compliance information requirements?","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"To expose the data to the agent, create: a custom connector","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nTo expose the data to the agent, create: a custom connector\nA custom connector enables the AI agent to securely connect to enterprise systems, APIs, and external\nbusiness applications that contain the R&D product specifications and compliance information. It provides a\nreusable and standardized integration mechanism that allows the agent to retrieve trusted organizational data\nduring interactions.\nAdd to the agent: a tool\nA tool allows the AI agent to interact with connected systems and use the exposed data during conversations\nand workflows. By adding a tool, the agent can dynamically retrieve product specifications, access\ncompliance information, and perform business operations required by users in real time.\nIncorrect Answers:\nan Azure AI Bot Service channel\nBot Service channels are used for communication endpoints and user interaction channels, not for exposing\nenterprise data.\na custom OData entity\nOData entities structure data access but are not the primary recommended mechanism for integrating\nenterprise systems with AI agents.\nthe Semantic Kernel\nSemantic Kernel is used for AI orchestration and workflow composition rather than exposing enterprise\nbusiness data.\nan event trigger\nEvent triggers automate workflow execution but do not directly provide data access to the AI agent.\nthe MCP server\nThe MCP server standardizes communication between models and tools but is not itself a data exposure\nmechanism.\na REST API\nREST APIs expose services, but the recommended approach is to connect them to the agent through\nconnectors and tools.","sourcePages":[111,112],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":101,"stem":"Which two components for the custom AI agent should you include in the application lifecycle\nmanagement (ALM) process?","rawQuestion":"Which two components for the custom AI agent should you include in the application lifecycle\nmanagement (ALM) process?\nA. an Azure package\nB. a ZIP package\nC. a Microsoft Power Platform solution\nD. a Cloud Scale Unit (CSU) package\nE. an X++ model","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"an Azure package"},{"label":"B","text":"a ZIP package"},{"label":"C","text":"a Microsoft Power Platform solution"},{"label":"D","text":"a Cloud Scale Unit (CSU) package"},{"label":"E","text":"an X++ model"}],"correct":["B","C"],"answer":"B. a ZIP package\nC. a Microsoft Power Platform solution","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nB. a ZIP package\nIn Microsoft Copilot Studio and Power Platform ALM processes, solutions are commonly exported and\nmoved between environments as ZIP packages. This enables version control, deployment automation,\nbackup, and consistent release management across development, testing, and production environments.\nC. a Microsoft Power Platform solution\nMicrosoft Power Platform solutions are the primary ALM container for Copilot Studio components, flows,\nconnectors, and related assets. Solutions help package, transport, manage, and deploy AI agent components\nin a structured and governed way throughout the lifecycle process.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. an Azure package\nAzure packages are not the standard ALM packaging mechanism for Copilot Studio and Power Platform\nsolutions.\nD. a Cloud Scale Unit (CSU) package\nCSU packages are related to Dynamics 365 Commerce scale units and are not relevant to AI agent ALM.\nE. an X++ model\nX++ models are used in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations development, not in Copilot Studio AI agent\nlifecycle management.","sourcePages":[112,113],"gradable":true,"multiple":true},{"id":102,"stem":"Which tools should you recommend to assist the CISO and the CIO with their specific responsibilities?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate tools to the correct executives. Each tool may be used once, more than\nonce, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"Which tools should you recommend to assist the CISO and the CIO with their specific responsibilities?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate tools to the correct executives. Each tool may be used once, more than\nonce, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Microsoft Purview for the CISO","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nMicrosoft Purview for the CISO\nThe Contoso case study states that the CISO must discover and inventory AI resources for auditing and\nidentify how much sensitive data was accessed during AI agent interactions and who accessed it. Microsoft\nPurview provides AI governance, compliance management, auditing, sensitivity labeling awareness, and data\naccess tracking capabilities. It enables the CISO to monitor AI-related data usage, audit user interactions, and\nidentify potential security risks involving sensitive information.\nMicrosoft Purview for the CIO\nThe CIO is responsible for ensuring that appropriate security labels are assigned to the data used by the AI\nagents. Microsoft Purview provides data classification, sensitivity labeling, information protection, and\ngovernance capabilities that allow the organization to apply and manage security labels across enterprise\ndata sources. This ensures that AI agents interact with data according to organizational compliance and\nsecurity requirements.\nIncorrect Answers:\nAzure Blob Storage\nBlob Storage is a storage service and does not provide AI governance, security labeling, or auditing\ncapabilities.\nAzure Resource Graph Explorer\nResource Graph Explorer inventories Azure infrastructure resources but does not provide sensitive data\nauditing, AI interaction monitoring, or security label management.\nCopilot Studio\nCopilot Studio is used to build and manage AI agents, but enterprise-wide data governance and compliance\nresponsibilities are handled by Microsoft Purview.","sourcePages":[113,114],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":103,"stem":"What should you configure for the custom AI agent?","rawQuestion":"What should you configure for the custom AI agent?\nA. AI-assisted evaluators\nB. classic orchestration\nC. generative orchestration\nD. Azure OpenAI reasoning models","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"AI-assisted evaluators"},{"label":"B","text":"classic orchestration"},{"label":"C","text":"generative orchestration"},{"label":"D","text":"Azure OpenAI reasoning models"}],"correct":["C"],"answer":"C. generative orchestration","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nC. generative orchestration\nThe Contoso case study states that the custom AI agent topics must be selected based on the description\nand purpose of the query rather than trigger phrases, making the interactions more conversational.\nGenerative orchestration enables AI-driven intent understanding and dynamic routing of conversations based\non semantic meaning instead of predefined keywords or trigger phrases. This directly supports\nconversational and natural language interactions.\nThe case study also mentions that the agent must eventually connect to other agents selected based on their\ndescription. Generative orchestration is designed to support intelligent agent selection and dynamic\norchestration between multiple agents.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. AI-assisted evaluators\nAI-assisted evaluators are used for assessment and quality evaluation rather than conversational routing or\norchestration.\nB. classic orchestration\nClassic orchestration relies primarily on predefined trigger phrases and deterministic routing, which does not\nmeet the conversational requirements.\nD. Azure OpenAI reasoning models\nReasoning models improve complex reasoning capabilities but do not specifically provide conversational\ntopic orchestration and dynamic agent routing.","sourcePages":[114,115],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":104,"stem":"Which Copilot Studio analytics metrics should you recommend to assist the executives with their\nspecific responsibilities? To answer, drag the appropriate metrics to the correct executives. Each metric may\nbe used once, more than once, or not at all.","rawQuestion":"Which Copilot Studio analytics metrics should you recommend to assist the executives with their\nspecific responsibilities? To answer, drag the appropriate metrics to the correct executives. Each metric may\nbe used once, more than once, or not at all.","choices":[],"correct":[],"answer":"Effectiveness","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nEffectiveness\nThe Contoso case study states that the CFO wants to compare abandoned AI interactions against resolved\nconversations to determine whether Copilot Studio credits are being used efficiently. In Copilot Studio\nanalytics, metrics such as resolution rate, abandonment rate, and escalation trends are part of the\nEffectiveness category. These metrics directly help measure whether AI conversations successfully resolve\nuser needs or consume credits inefficiently through abandoned sessions.\nSatisfaction\nThe CTO wants to track user feedback on the quality of AI agent responses and identify consistently poor\nexperiences that may require reengineering. The Satisfaction metric measures customer sentiment, ratings,\nCSAT feedback, and user experience quality during AI interactions. This makes it the most appropriate metric\nfor monitoring response quality and identifying problematic conversations.\nIncorrect Answers:\nUse\nUse metrics track interaction volume, active sessions, and engagement trends, but they do not specifically\nmeasure abandoned versus resolved conversations.\nTool use\nTool use metrics focus on how frequently tools, plugins, connectors, or external integrations are invoked by\nthe AI agent rather than user satisfaction or conversation effectiveness.","sourcePages":[115,116],"gradable":false,"multiple":false},{"id":105,"stem":"What should you recommend to assist the CTO with the prebuilt agent selection process?","rawQuestion":"What should you recommend to assist the CTO with the prebuilt agent selection process?\nA. Agent management\nB. Copilot Studio\nC. Lifecycle Services (LCS)\nD. Immersive Home","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"Agent management"},{"label":"B","text":"Copilot Studio"},{"label":"C","text":"Lifecycle Services (LCS)"},{"label":"D","text":"Immersive Home"}],"correct":["A"],"answer":"A. Agent management","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nA. Agent management\nThe Contoso case study states that the CTO has not yet selected a prebuilt AI agent for Dynamics 365 Supply\nChain Management and wants to view available agent templates and preview their capabilities during\ndiscovery meetings with stakeholders. Agent management is the administrative workspace within Dynamics\n365 Finance and Operations applications that allows administrators and technical leaders to discover,\nconfigure, evaluate, and manage first-party AI agents and templates before deployment.\nIt provides visibility into available agent capabilities and supports the onboarding and configuration process\nfor Dynamics 365 AI agents, making it the most appropriate tool for the CTO's selection and evaluation\nrequirements.\nIncorrect Answers:\nB. Copilot Studio\nCopilot Studio is used primarily for creating and customizing low-code AI agents rather than browsing and\nevaluating prebuilt Dynamics 365 ERP AI agent templates.\nC. Lifecycle Services (LCS)\nLifecycle Services supports Dynamics 365 environment deployment, updates, and lifecycle operations, but it\ndoes not provide prebuilt AI agent discovery and preview functionality.\nD. Immersive Home\nImmersive Home is designed for operational experiences and process visibility rather than administrative\nmanagement and evaluation of prebuilt AI agent templates.","sourcePages":[116,117],"gradable":true,"multiple":false},{"id":106,"stem":"What should you recommend to assist the CEO with their specific responsibilities?","rawQuestion":"What should you recommend to assist the CEO with their specific responsibilities?\nA. the Microsoft Service Trust Portal\nB. Microsoft Foundry Tools\nC. Microsoft Purview\nD. the Responsible AI dashboard\nE. Compliance Center","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"the Microsoft Service Trust Portal"},{"label":"B","text":"Microsoft Foundry Tools"},{"label":"C","text":"Microsoft Purview"},{"label":"D","text":"the Responsible AI dashboard"},{"label":"E","text":"Compliance Center"}],"correct":["D"],"answer":"D. the Responsible AI dashboard","explanation":"Correct Answers:\nD. the Responsible AI dashboard\nThe Contoso case study states that the CEO wants quarterly assessments that verify reliability,\ninterpretability, fairness, and compliance using Microsoft-recommended tools. These are core Responsible AI\nprinciples. The Responsible AI dashboard is specifically designed to evaluate and monitor AI systems against\nresponsible AI metrics such as fairness, explainability, reliability, transparency, and model behavior analysis.\nThis directly supports the CEO's requirement to ensure that all AI solutions adhere to industry-standard\nresponsible AI practices.\nIncorrect Answers:\nA. the Microsoft Service Trust Portal\nThe Service Trust Portal provides compliance documentation and audit reports but does not evaluate AI\nmodels for fairness, interpretability, or reliability.\nB. Microsoft Foundry Tools\nMicrosoft Foundry Tools support AI development and orchestration but are not specifically designed as a\nResponsible AI assessment dashboard.\nC. Microsoft Purview\nMicrosoft Purview focuses on governance, compliance, auditing, and data protection rather than Responsible\nAI metric evaluation.\nE. Compliance Center\nCompliance Center manages compliance policies and regulatory controls but does not provide Responsible\nAI assessment capabilities.","sourcePages":[117,118],"gradable":true,"multiple":false}];
